{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n '\"'^#{1,5} |'\"\\\\b(F[1-9]|F1[0-2]|DX[1-8]|P7|I1|L8)\\\\b' docs/02-product-spec.md docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md\"","aggregated_output":"docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:1:# Phase 2 plan: Axylo 1.0 in TypeScript\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:5:This plan builds the product defined in [02-product-spec.md](02-product-spec.md). Criterion IDs (F1, L1, A2, DX4, P1 …) refer to that spec; finding IDs (R5, D2 …) refer to [01-landscape.md](01-landscape.md).\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:9:## 1. Public API\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:13:### 1.1 Shape\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:34:| One function per chart type (`line`, `area`, `bar`, `donut`, `scatter`) | Each has its own options type, so options that do not apply are type errors (DX3), and bundlers can drop unused chart types. Whether shared code actually tree-shakes is measured on real consumer builds in M7. |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:38:### 1.2 Data: one logical table, three forms\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:60:| Order | Line, area and scatter are sorted by x internally (F11). `append` expects rows at or after the last x. |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:63:### 1.3 Fields\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:82:| `type` (x) | `'date'`, `'time'`, `'number'`, `'category'`. Inferred when omitted, except that a number is never inferred to be an instant (F4). |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:84:| `format`, `tickFormat` | Readouts and ticks are formatted separately (F6). |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:85:| `scale` (value) | `'percent'` (0–100) or `'ratio'` (0–1). Declared, never inferred (F7). |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:86:| `missing` (value) | `'gap'` (default) or `'zero'` (F2). |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:88:| `domain` | Author bounds. Widened to zero on bar and area (F1). |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:90:### 1.4 Common options\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:101:| `colors` | A fixed color per series name (DX6). |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:102:| `messages`, `locale` | Built-in text and formatting locale (A10, F6). |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:107:### 1.5 Handle and commit contract\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:130:### 1.6 Diagnostics\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:143:The reader of that chart sees \"Values not shown: 3. See table.\" (F12).\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:149:### 1.7 React adapter (not in 1.0)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:169:## 2. Architecture\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:171:### 2.1 Pipeline\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:181:The **frame** is the normalized, lossless data: typed columns, a validity bitmap, dictionary-encoded categories, source-row indexes and a revision number. It is the only source for exact values. The table, the summary, keyboard navigation, tooltips and events read the frame. The **scene** is geometry for drawing and may be simplified (F5); nothing reads values back from it. The first draft derived accessibility from the scene, and two reviewers showed that a simplified drawing cannot return the samples it dropped.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:186:| view model | Scales, visible index ranges, series visibility | Domain rules implement F1 and F7. |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:188:| layout | Plot rectangle, tick sets, label boxes, legend and control placement | Implements L1, L3, L8. Takes a text-measurement interface as input. |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:192:### 2.2 Invalidation\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:206:### 2.3 Rendering\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:210:**This is reopened and decided in M1 by measurement.** The first draft justified canvas by Phase 1's slow SVG libraries, but those create a node per point; the one-path-per-series SVG chart in the harness was fast. With drawing simplified to a few vertices per pixel column (F5), an SVG path per series is small. SVG follows CSS variables and class-toggled themes with no repaint code, adapts to forced colors, prints sharply, needs no pixel-ratio handling and allocates no backing store (a 1,920×1,080 canvas at pixel ratio 2 is about 33 MB). Canvas is likely cheaper for per-frame redraw when streaming and hovering.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:214:**Simplified drawing.** Per device-pixel column and per contiguous run of valid samples, the stroke covers the run's minimum and maximum (F5). One rule for when it applies: more than four visible samples per device-pixel column on average. The pass over visible samples is O(visible) on typed columns and is measured separately from drawing; the vertex count is bounded by width times the number of runs, not by width alone.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:218:### 2.4 Document structure\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:241:### 2.5 Repository\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:257:## 3. Milestones\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:261:### M0. Foundations (ceiling: 5 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:270:### M1. A vertical slice, and the two open decisions (ceiling: 12 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:286:### M2. Faithful data, on the line chart (ceiling: 8 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:288:- The diagnostic registry (F3, F12); missing values and absent steps (F2); the three temporal types (F4); formatting (F6); domains (F7); sorting and duplicates (F11); `validate()`.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:292:- F4's four fixture variants in three zones, plus DST and leap-day cases.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:293:- Property tests: no scene path contains a non-finite coordinate; no segment crosses a missing index; domains obey F7.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:298:### M3. Reading the chart (ceiling: 10 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:300:- Title, `labelledBy`, subtitle, axis titles; toolbar with legend toggles; full tooltip and fixed readout (I1); label fitting (L1) and the too-small fallback (L8); states (L7); reference lines and bands.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:311:### M4. Accessibility, complete on the line chart (ceiling: 10 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:324:### M5. Bar, area, donut, scatter and the compact variant (ceiling: 18 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:338:### M6. Updates and scale (ceiling: 10 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:343:- Sequence tests from DX5.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:344:- F5's assertions on kept samples, including the adversarial same-column cases.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:346:- Budgets P1–P5 and P7 measured with the Phase 3 method, recalibrated once if M1's provisional values were wrong, then frozen with a written reason for each.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:350:### M7. Distribution and documentation (ceiling: 8 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:355:- The packed tarball installed into two clean fixtures (npm with a bundler, script tag): the first chart works in ten lines or fewer in each (DX1).\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:356:- Node import and `validate()` without a DOM; strict CSP with real headers (DX8).\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:357:- Size measured on the packed distribution for line-only, full and script-tag cases, CSS included (DX7).\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:361:### M8. Release (ceiling: 10 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:372:## 4. Test strategy\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:377:| Property | fast-check | Invariants that must hold for any data: F1, F2, F5, F7, F11, L1. |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:382:| Types | `docs/api/examples.ts` pattern | The listed mistakes fail to compile (DX3). |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:383:| Packed distribution | Clean install fixtures | What users actually get (DX1, DX7, DX8, DX10). |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:388:## 5. Risks\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:396:| Layout promises collide in small tiles. | L8 defines the fallback; the property test covers down to 240 px. |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:400:## 6. What waits until after 1.0\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:406:| Second y axis | Often misleading; needs a design that keeps F1. |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:1:# Phase 1b: Axylo product specification\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:7:## 1. What Axylo is\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:15:### 1.1 Why someone would choose it\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:29:### 1.2 Who it is for\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:35:### 1.3 Principles\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:46:## 2. Scope of the first release\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:48:### 2.1 Chart types\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:65:### 2.2 Workflows\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:76:### 2.3 Supported environments\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:86:## 3. Acceptance criteria: the reader's experience\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:90:### 3.1 Faithful representation\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:94:| F1 | Bar and area value axes always include zero. An author `domain` that excludes zero is widened and raises AX110. | Property test on domains, including all-negative and mixed-sign data; `bars` fixture (values 82–97) starts at 0 | R1 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:95:| F2 | Missing values are never drawn as zero and never bridged unless the author asks. An explicit `null` is a gap, silently. An isolated value between two gaps is a visible point. A missing bar shows a \"no data\" mark at the baseline, distinct from a zero bar. On a time or date axis with a regular step, an absent step is a gap and raises AX105; `missing: 'zero'` on the field turns absent and null values into zeros for counts. Tooltip, table and announcements say \"No data\". | `trend` with nulls and `trend` with the same 7 rows deleted render the same gap; `dirty` fixtures; property test that no segment connects across a missing index | R5 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:96:| F3 | Input that cannot be plotted as given is reported, never guessed. The diagnostic registry (section 3.5) fixes each case's level, what is drawn and what the reader sees. | One test per code; the combined `dirty` fixture and the four one-bad-value fixtures yield exactly the codes listed in section 3.5 | R5, D5 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:97:| F4 | Three temporal types, never mixed silently. **Calendar dates** (`2026-02-24`, `2026-02`, `2026`) read the same in every viewer time zone. **Instants** (epoch milliseconds declared `type: 'time'`, `Date` objects, ISO strings with an offset) are shown in `timeZone` (default: the viewer's), with the zone named in the tooltip heading and the description. **Wall-clock times** (date-time strings without an offset) are shown as written, with hint AX112. Instants that all fall on the same time of day, whole days apart, raise hint AX111 (\"these look like calendar dates\"). A bare number is never assumed to be an instant. | Four fixture variants in UTC, America/Los_Angeles and Asia/Kolkata: (a) date strings: identical text in all three; (b) ISO strings with `Z`, (c) epoch ms with `type: 'time'`, (d) `Date` objects: each zone's correct local text, zone visible, AX111 raised; with `timeZone: 'UTC'` or `type: 'date'`: identical. Plus: both DST transitions, a leap day, an unparseable date | R3 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:98:| F5 | No smoothing. When a line is denser than the screen, the drawing is simplified with this guarantee: for every device-pixel column, and every contiguous run of valid samples inside it, the drawn stroke covers exactly that run's minimum and maximum. A break between two runs is always kept, even when both fall in one pixel column; a slot a series merely has no row for (weekly data beside daily) does not break its line. Stacked areas are simplified on their cumulative sums. Exact values always come from the data, not the drawing. | Assertions on which source samples the drawing kept, for: one outlier in 1,000,000; two peaks and a gap in one column; alternating valid and missing samples; tied extremes; device pixel ratio 1 and 2; several zoom levels. A sample that the drawing dropped is still reachable by keyboard and in the table | D8, D9 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:99:| F6 | Numbers and dates are formatted for the page locale. Ticks and readouts are formatted separately: ticks may be compact (`50K`); tooltip, table and announcements show the full integer part, and the fraction with up to six significant digits not counting the fraction's leading zeros (`123,456.75`; `0.000123456`; `0.1 + 0.2` reads `0.3`), with the unit. A custom formatter that throws falls back to the default and raises a diagnostic. | Expected strings for chosen values (large integers, small decimals, percentages, zoned times) in `en-US`, `de-DE` and `en-IN` | R4 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:100:| F7 | Domains follow one order of precedence: the zoomed view is inside the author's `domain` if given, otherwise inside the automatic domain. The automatic domain contains all data, the reference lines and bands the chart draws (a compact chart draws none, so `reference` has no effect there and is reported as an option that does not apply) and stack totals, and ends at the first tick at or beyond each extreme. A field declared `scale: 'percent'` (0–100) or `'ratio'` (0–1) ends at full scale only when its maximum is at least half of full scale and not above it. Nothing is inferred from a label. | Property tests; `bars` ends at 100 when declared `percent`; a 4% maximum ends at the first tick at or above 4% | R1 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:101:| F8 | Donut: slices keep the given order, start at 12 o'clock and run clockwise. The legend shows every slice's value and percentage as text, in slice order. A zero slice is listed without an arc; a `null` slice is listed as \"No data\". A negative value is AX120 and the chart shows its error state. With more than eight slices that have an arc (a positive value), the seven largest are drawn and the rest of those are grouped as \"Other (n)\", with AX121; zero and `null` slices never count toward the limit and are never grouped, so each stays listed on its own; the table lists every category. Shares are shown with at most one fraction digit. An all-zero donut shows the empty state. | `parts` fixture; one test per case | R7, landscape scoreboard 3.1 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:102:| F9 | Up to eight series, no two share a color. Beyond eight, each series still has a unique combination of color and dash or marker (for bars: color and fill pattern, which gives 32 distinct combinations; beyond 32 bar series combinations repeat), and AX130 is raised. | Tests with 6, 8 and 12 series | R7 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:103:| F10 | Stacks place positive and negative values on their own sides of zero, and the axis covers both totals. The readout's total is the sum when every component has the same sign; when signs are mixed it gives the positive total and the negative total, the two ends the reader sees, not their net. A missing component is drawn with zero height and the stack is marked \"incomplete\" in tooltip, table and announcements. | Property test on stacked geometry; mixed-sign and incomplete fixtures | R5 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:104:| F11 | Line, area and scatter are drawn in x order whatever the input order. On line, area and bar, two rows with the same x in one series are AX108 and that series is not drawn. On scatter, points sharing an x (or both coordinates) are ordinary data. | Shuffled `trend` renders identically; duplicate fixture | R5 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:105:| F12 | When a warning means values were left out or changed, the chart says so to the reader: a short note (\"Values not shown: 2. See table.\", worded so it reads correctly for any count) inside the figure and in the accessibility tree, in every build. | Production build given `dirty` shows the note | R5, R6 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:107:### 3.2 Legibility\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:118:| L8 | When the figure cannot hold a readable plot (less than 120×80 CSS px for the plot), it shows the title, a short summary (the first sentence of the generated summary, on at most two lines, cut with an ellipsis beyond that; the full summary remains the figure's description) and the table control instead, and raises AX142. | Narrow and short container tests | R2 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:120:### 3.3 Interaction\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:124:| I1 | Pointing anywhere in the plot shows what is there, by the picking rule for the chart type (section 3.6), within one frame at the 95th percentile. On line, area and bar the tooltip lists every visible series at the nearest x; a series with no row at that x reads \"No observation\", distinct from \"No data\". The tooltip stays inside the viewport, does not cover the active mark or any control, can itself be hovered, and lists at most eight rows plus \"+N more\". In a plot too small for it (decision 0004: narrower than 240 px or shorter than 140 px) the same content appears as text in a strip reserved under the plot. Escape closes it until the pointer reaches a different datum. | Hover at 20 positions including all four edges; sparse-series fixture; narrow tile with eight series; Escape test | R15 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:131:### 3.4 Accessibility\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:140:| A4 | \"View as table\" shows the data as an HTML table built from the normalized data when opened: the title as caption, field labels with units as column headers, x values as row headers, values formatted as in F6. It covers the current x range, 100 rows per page, with every row reachable by paging or by jumping to an x value. It sits in a focusable scroll region no taller than the plot, and opening it does not move other charts. | Table content compared with fixture data, including a value near row 900,000 of 1,000,000; a 24-chart page layout test | R13 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:148:### 3.5 Diagnostic registry\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:150:Level decides delivery. **Error**: the chart or a series cannot be drawn; the chart shows its error state. **Warning**: something is not drawn as given; the reader sees the note in F12. **Hint**: advice for the author. Errors and warnings print once per code per page in every build; hints print in development builds only. `strict: true` throws on errors and warnings found while validating a create, `update` or `append` call, synchronously from that call. A diagnostic that can only arise later (a contrast check after the page's CSS changes, a custom formatter that throws when a table page is built, a label that stops fitting after a resize) is reported when it is found and is never thrown. Every diagnostic names the chart; those about data name the field and the first row indexes in the caller's own data.\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:184:Expected results for the Phase 1 fixtures: the combined `dirty` fixture (`[10, 20, null, NaN, '40', undefined, 70]`) yields AX102, AX103 and AX106 once each, draws Mon–Tue as a segment and Sun as a point, and shows the F12 note. `dirty-string` (one `'40'` among numbers) yields AX106. A column made only of numeric strings yields AX104 and is drawn in full. `dirty-null` yields nothing.\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:188:### 3.6 Type matrix\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:196:| Simplified drawing when dense | F5 | F5 on cumulative sums | No; more bars than pixels raises a hint | No | No in 1.0; above 50,000 points a hint says drawing may be slow |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:200:### 3.7 Compact variant\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:207:| C2 | The fidelity rules still hold: zero baseline for bar and area (F1), gaps (F2), diagnostics (F3), simplified drawing (F5), sorting (F11), no motion (I5), scheme from the page (L4). | The same fixtures as the full charts |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:210:| C5 | When values were left out (F12), the description says so and a small marker with a text equivalent appears in the corner. | `dirty` fixture in compact mode |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:214:### 3.8 What the evidence will and will not show\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:218:## 4. Acceptance criteria: the developer's experience\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:222:| DX1 | The first chart takes ten lines or fewer, including import, by npm and by script tag. No required option besides data and fields. | Two CI tests that render the documented snippet from the packed distribution | D1 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:223:| DX2 | One logical table, three ways to pass it: long rows, wide rows, typed columns. The three forms of each fixture produce the same normalized data and the same diagnostics, with row indexes in the caller's own form. | Equivalence tests | D2 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:224:| DX3 | With typed rows, a misspelled field, a text field on a value axis, wide `y` combined with `series`, and an option that does not apply to the chart type all fail to compile. At run time, for untyped rows, the same mistakes raise AX101, AX106 and AX150. | [docs/api/examples.ts](api/examples.ts) compiles and each marked mistake fails (already checked with TypeScript 7.0.2); runtime tests per code | D5 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:225:| DX4 | Diagnostics follow the registry in section 3.5: stable code, chart name, field and rows where they apply, the fix, and a documentation page per code. | Message snapshots; link checker | R5, D5 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:226:| DX5 | Three methods change a chart: `update` changes options, `append` adds rows, `zoom` sets the view. They apply in call order. Validation and diagnostics are synchronous; drawing is deferred to the next frame; marks, labels, hit testing and the accessibility layer always switch to a new state together. `destroy()` releases everything and cancels pending work. No `resize()` exists. | Sequence tests: replace then append then zoom in one turn; a rejected `strict` update leaves the last valid chart; destroy before the frame; 1,000 create–destroy cycles show no heap growth trend (P7) | D3, R16 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:227:| DX6 | Colors, font and spacing are CSS custom properties with documented names; the chart inherits `font-family` and `color`. `colors` pins a color to a series name so the same series has the same color on every chart. There is no theme object and no positional palette option. | Theming tests, including a 24-tile page where series subsets differ | R10 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:228:| DX7 | Zero runtime dependencies (reconsidered only with a measured comparison). Size targets, set by the owner on 2026-11-18 after measurement: 96 KiB gzipped for everything and 80 KiB for a page that uses only `line`, CSS included. (The first targets, 40 and 25 KiB, were written before anything was built; an analysis of the built library showed they cannot hold the accessibility layer, the table, diagnostics, three kinds of time and streaming: `reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/size.analysis.md`.) | size-limit in CI on the packed distribution | D4 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:229:| DX8 | Importing in Node without a DOM does not throw, and `validate()` runs there. Creating, destroying and creating again on the same element works and leaks nothing (the sequence a framework's strict or development mode produces). Works under a Content Security Policy without `unsafe-inline` or `unsafe-eval`, served with real headers. Labels and formatter output are inserted as text, never as HTML. A target that matches no element throws an error that names the selector. | CI tests | D1 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:235:## 5. Performance budgets\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:246:| P6 | Download | Minified and gzipped bytes, CSS included | Exact | DX7 | 01, section 1.4 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:248:| P7 | Memory | Bytes owned by Axylo beyond the caller's columns for 1,000,000 points; heap trend over 1,000 create–destroy cycles | Measured after forced collection | At most 1× the size of the caller's two columns; no upward slope | Not measured in Phase 1 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:256:## 6. Decisions and the findings behind them\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:281:## 7. Changes this revision rejected\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:290:## 8. Decisions still open\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
