{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '86,251p' docs/02-product-spec.md\nsed -n '107,129p;169,257p' docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md\ncat docs/decisions/0004-tooltip-and-focused-text.md\"","aggregated_output":"## 3. Acceptance criteria: the reader's experience\n\nEvery criterion maps to at least one test in the acceptance matrix kept in the repository (criterion → test → chart types it applies to → result). \"Property test\" means the statement is checked over at least 1,000 generated datasets. Expected values come from the fixture data and this specification, never from the implementation's output.\n\n### 3.1 Faithful representation\n\n| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| F1 | Bar and area value axes always include zero. An author `domain` that excludes zero is widened and raises AX110. | Property test on domains, including all-negative and mixed-sign data; `bars` fixture (values 82–97) starts at 0 | R1 |\n| F2 | Missing values are never drawn as zero and never bridged unless the author asks. An explicit `null` is a gap, silently. An isolated value between two gaps is a visible point. A missing bar shows a \"no data\" mark at the baseline, distinct from a zero bar. On a time or date axis with a regular step, an absent step is a gap and raises AX105; `missing: 'zero'` on the field turns absent and null values into zeros for counts. Tooltip, table and announcements say \"No data\". | `trend` with nulls and `trend` with the same 7 rows deleted render the same gap; `dirty` fixtures; property test that no segment connects across a missing index | R5 |\n| F3 | Input that cannot be plotted as given is reported, never guessed. The diagnostic registry (section 3.5) fixes each case's level, what is drawn and what the reader sees. | One test per code; the combined `dirty` fixture and the four one-bad-value fixtures yield exactly the codes listed in section 3.5 | R5, D5 |\n| F4 | Three temporal types, never mixed silently. **Calendar dates** (`2026-02-24`, `2026-02`, `2026`) read the same in every viewer time zone. **Instants** (epoch milliseconds declared `type: 'time'`, `Date` objects, ISO strings with an offset) are shown in `timeZone` (default: the viewer's), with the zone named in the tooltip heading and the description. **Wall-clock times** (date-time strings without an offset) are shown as written, with hint AX112. Instants that all fall on the same time of day, whole days apart, raise hint AX111 (\"these look like calendar dates\"). A bare number is never assumed to be an instant. | Four fixture variants in UTC, America/Los_Angeles and Asia/Kolkata: (a) date strings: identical text in all three; (b) ISO strings with `Z`, (c) epoch ms with `type: 'time'`, (d) `Date` objects: each zone's correct local text, zone visible, AX111 raised; with `timeZone: 'UTC'` or `type: 'date'`: identical. Plus: both DST transitions, a leap day, an unparseable date | R3 |\n| F5 | No smoothing. When a line is denser than the screen, the drawing is simplified with this guarantee: for every device-pixel column, and every contiguous run of valid samples inside it, the drawn stroke covers exactly that run's minimum and maximum. A break between two runs is always kept, even when both fall in one pixel column; a slot a series merely has no row for (weekly data beside daily) does not break its line. Stacked areas are simplified on their cumulative sums. Exact values always come from the data, not the drawing. | Assertions on which source samples the drawing kept, for: one outlier in 1,000,000; two peaks and a gap in one column; alternating valid and missing samples; tied extremes; device pixel ratio 1 and 2; several zoom levels. A sample that the drawing dropped is still reachable by keyboard and in the table | D8, D9 |\n| F6 | Numbers and dates are formatted for the page locale. Ticks and readouts are formatted separately: ticks may be compact (`50K`); tooltip, table and announcements show the full integer part, and the fraction with up to six significant digits not counting the fraction's leading zeros (`123,456.75`; `0.000123456`; `0.1 + 0.2` reads `0.3`), with the unit. A custom formatter that throws falls back to the default and raises a diagnostic. | Expected strings for chosen values (large integers, small decimals, percentages, zoned times) in `en-US`, `de-DE` and `en-IN` | R4 |\n| F7 | Domains follow one order of precedence: the zoomed view is inside the author's `domain` if given, otherwise inside the automatic domain. The automatic domain contains all data, the reference lines and bands the chart draws (a compact chart draws none, so `reference` has no effect there and is reported as an option that does not apply) and stack totals, and ends at the first tick at or beyond each extreme. A field declared `scale: 'percent'` (0–100) or `'ratio'` (0–1) ends at full scale only when its maximum is at least half of full scale and not above it. Nothing is inferred from a label. | Property tests; `bars` ends at 100 when declared `percent`; a 4% maximum ends at the first tick at or above 4% | R1 |\n| F8 | Donut: slices keep the given order, start at 12 o'clock and run clockwise. The legend shows every slice's value and percentage as text, in slice order. A zero slice is listed without an arc; a `null` slice is listed as \"No data\". A negative value is AX120 and the chart shows its error state. With more than eight slices that have an arc (a positive value), the seven largest are drawn and the rest of those are grouped as \"Other (n)\", with AX121; zero and `null` slices never count toward the limit and are never grouped, so each stays listed on its own; the table lists every category. Shares are shown with at most one fraction digit. An all-zero donut shows the empty state. | `parts` fixture; one test per case | R7, landscape scoreboard 3.1 |\n| F9 | Up to eight series, no two share a color. Beyond eight, each series still has a unique combination of color and dash or marker (for bars: color and fill pattern, which gives 32 distinct combinations; beyond 32 bar series combinations repeat), and AX130 is raised. | Tests with 6, 8 and 12 series | R7 |\n| F10 | Stacks place positive and negative values on their own sides of zero, and the axis covers both totals. The readout's total is the sum when every component has the same sign; when signs are mixed it gives the positive total and the negative total, the two ends the reader sees, not their net. A missing component is drawn with zero height and the stack is marked \"incomplete\" in tooltip, table and announcements. | Property test on stacked geometry; mixed-sign and incomplete fixtures | R5 |\n| F11 | Line, area and scatter are drawn in x order whatever the input order. On line, area and bar, two rows with the same x in one series are AX108 and that series is not drawn. On scatter, points sharing an x (or both coordinates) are ordinary data. | Shuffled `trend` renders identically; duplicate fixture | R5 |\n| F12 | When a warning means values were left out or changed, the chart says so to the reader: a short note (\"Values not shown: 2. See table.\", worded so it reads correctly for any count) inside the figure and in the accessibility tree, in every build. | Production build given `dirty` shows the note | R5, R6 |\n\n### 3.2 Legibility\n\n| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| L1 | No visible text overlaps other text or is clipped, and no label disappears without the reader being told. Remedies, in order: thin (time, date, numeric and `ordered` category axes only); wrap to at most three lines; rotate up to 45°; truncate in the middle, keeping every label distinct, with the full text in tooltip and table. If labels still do not fit, a visible note (\"Some labels hidden. See table.\") appears and AX143 suggests horizontal bars. Axylo never changes a chart's orientation on its own. | In real browsers, after fonts load: for widths 240–1,920 px and generated label sets, zero intersecting text boxes (1 px tolerance), zero text outside the figure, truncated labels pairwise distinct. Repeated with the WCAG 1.4.12 text-spacing overrides, 200% text-only zoom, and a font that loads late | R2 |\n| L2 | Text is at least 12 CSS px and at least 4.5:1 against what is behind it, including text drawn inside a mark and legend entries for hidden series. Marks, axis lines and focus indicators are at least 3:1. Gridlines are exempt: they are not needed to read a value while the tooltip and table exist. Holds in light and dark. | Computed-style and rendered-pixel checks in both schemes | R7, R10 |\n| L3 | The chart follows its container with no call from the page, in both directions. With no definite height, the plot is 16:9, clamped between 160 and 480 CSS px tall, and the title, controls and table flow above and below it. A container that starts hidden or zero-sized draws when it gets a size. | Resize 720 → 360 → 720 px; hidden-then-shown test; `height: auto` container is stable over 10 frames | R16, D1 |\n| L4 | Light or dark is taken from the chart element's computed `color-scheme`; if that is not set, from the luminance of the inherited text color. `--axylo-background` overrides both. Marks repaint within one frame when the scheme, forced colors, contrast preference, or the `class`, `style` or `data-theme` of `<html>` or `<body>` changes. Forced-colors mode uses system colors. | A page that sets `color-scheme`; a class-toggled dark theme without it; a light-only page on a dark device (must stay light); a runtime toggle; forced-colors emulation | R10 |\n| L5 | A marker on every point appears only when adjacent points are at least 12 px apart (provisional value), and always for isolated points. | `trend` at 720 px has none; a 12-point line has them | R8 |\n| L6 | Title, subtitle and axis titles with units are part of the chart. A value axis is titled with its label (the field name when none is given) and unit. An x axis that is a measure (number) is titled the same way; a time, date or category x axis is titled only when the author gives a `label`, since its ticks already say what it is. A reference line or band shows its label next to it. Axis titles never overlap tick labels. | Layout tests; `trend` fixture | R9 |\n| L7 | Empty data shows a message inside the plot area; so do loading, error and Axylo's own internal failures. No invented axis ranges, no blank box. A change of state is announced once, politely. | `empty` fixture: message visible and in the accessibility tree, no tick labels; forced internal error shows the error state | R6 |\n| L8 | When the figure cannot hold a readable plot (less than 120×80 CSS px for the plot), it shows the title, a short summary (the first sentence of the generated summary, on at most two lines, cut with an ellipsis beyond that; the full summary remains the figure's description) and the table control instead, and raises AX142. | Narrow and short container tests | R2 |\n\n### 3.3 Interaction\n\n| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| I1 | Pointing anywhere in the plot shows what is there, by the picking rule for the chart type (section 3.6), within one frame at the 95th percentile. On line, area and bar the tooltip lists every visible series at the nearest x; a series with no row at that x reads \"No observation\", distinct from \"No data\". The tooltip stays inside the viewport, does not cover the active mark or any control, can itself be hovered, and lists at most eight rows plus \"+N more\". In a plot too small for it (decision 0004: narrower than 240 px or shorter than 140 px) the same content appears as text in a strip reserved under the plot. Escape closes it until the pointer reaches a different datum. | Hover at 20 positions including all four edges; sparse-series fixture; narrow tile with eight series; Escape test | R15 |\n| I2 | Legend entries are toggle buttons with a pressed state, operable by click, Enter and Space. | Keyboard and accessibility-tree tests | R12 |\n| I3 | Zoom on time, date and numeric x axes is reachable four ways: visible Zoom in, Zoom out and Reset buttons that work with single clicks; drag to select a range; `+`, `-`, `0` and Shift+arrows; and pinch when `zoom: 'touch'` is set. The wheel zooms only while the plot has focus, and never when Ctrl or Meta is held, so browser zoom and page scrolling are not intercepted. | Reach a given range by clicks only; by keyboard only; by drag; page scrolls normally with the pointer over an unfocused chart | D7, R12, WCAG 2.5.1, 2.5.7, 1.4.4 |\n| I4 | Every interaction works with a single pointer without dragging, and by touch. Every control is at least 24×24 CSS px. A one-finger drag on a chart scrolls the page. | Touch emulation; size checks | WCAG 2.5.7, 2.5.8 |\n| I5 | First render is not animated. An update may transition for at most 200 ms. Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` nothing transitions, on first render or on update. | After `document.fonts.ready`: first frame equals the frame at 2.5 s within a 0.1% pixel tolerance, in both settings; an update under reduced motion reaches its final frame in one step; a 24-chart filter change settles within 200 ms | R14 |\n| I6 | A chart fed by `append` shows a Pause/Resume control. While paused, new rows are buffered and the view does not move. | Stream test | WCAG 2.2.2 |\n\n### 3.4 Accessibility\n\nTarget: every WCAG 2.2 level AA success criterion that applies to what Axylo renders, as component-level evidence. Conformance of a page is a property of the page and remains the author's.\n\n| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A1 | Every chart has an accessible name: its `title`, or the heading named by `labelledBy`, or else a generated name (\"Line chart of Sessions by Day\") with AX140 raised in every build. The title's heading level is the author's (`level`), and it can be hidden visually. Every chart has a generated summary: type, series, x range with time zone, y range, each series' minimum, maximum and last value, and any omissions. `description` adds to the summary and never removes the zone or omission statements. Element ids are unique per chart. | Accessibility-tree snapshot for every fixture; a 24-chart page has no duplicate ids; axe `heading-order` | R11, R13 |\n| A2 | Keyboard: at most two tab stops while the table is closed, the plot and one toolbar with roving focus that holds legend toggles, zoom, pause and the table control. Every point is reachable in order; the series can be switched; the ends can be reached directly; focus can leave the chart. The focus indicator is at least 2 px and 3:1 and is not hidden by the tooltip. The exact keys and roles are fixed after the pattern spike in M1 (section 8). | Forward and reverse traversal in collapsed, zoomed, streaming and table-open states; focus after hiding the focused series; indicator contrast | R12 |\n| A3 | Moving focus to a point conveys its text (series, x, value with unit, position) through the focused element's accessible name or a polite live region. On touch screen readers, every point is reachable by swiping, or the table is reachable within two gestures. | Accessibility-tree snapshot after each key; a recorded rehearsal on NVDA with Chrome, NVDA with Firefox, VoiceOver with Safari on macOS, VoiceOver on iOS, and TalkBack with Chrome | R12, R13 |\n| A4 | \"View as table\" shows the data as an HTML table built from the normalized data when opened: the title as caption, field labels with units as column headers, x values as row headers, values formatted as in F6. It covers the current x range, 100 rows per page, with every row reachable by paging or by jumping to an x value. It sits in a focusable scroll region no taller than the plot, and opening it does not move other charts. | Table content compared with fixture data, including a value near row 900,000 of 1,000,000; a 24-chart page layout test | R13 |\n| A5 | Series are never distinguished by color alone, in any mode. Each line and area carries a shape marker at its last point and at sparse intervals, repeated in the legend and tooltip; under `prefers-contrast: more` or forced colors, lines also differ by dash. Adjacent filled marks (slices, stacked segments) are separated by at least 1 px of background. The default palette has eight colors, each at least 3:1 on the default light and dark backgrounds, with a minimum pairwise OKLab difference of 0.10 (provisional) under normal vision and simulated protanopia, deuteranopia and tritanopia. On any other background, Axylo measures the palette against the resolved background and raises AX141 below 3:1. | Palette script scoring the rendered marks of an eight-series chart in both schemes, repeats included; rendered-pixel checks of separators; a grayscale screenshot in which three crossing lines can be told apart by marker | R7, WCAG 1.4.1, 1.4.11 |\n| A6 | Content reflows at 320 CSS px and 400% zoom without two-dimensional scrolling. User text-spacing overrides do not clip text. | Viewport and style-override tests in three engines | WCAG 1.4.10, 1.4.12 |\n| A7 | Data arriving does not speak. Announcements happen only for the reader's own navigation, and for a change of state (L7). While data streams, keyboard focus stays on the same datum until it leaves the window. | Stream test counting live-region changes with and without focus; 24 concurrent streams | R12 |\n| A8 | axe-core reports no violations on any fixture in any state, including `heading-order` and `scrollable-region-focusable`. | CI | R11 |\n| A9 | A conformance table lists every applicable WCAG 2.2 AA success criterion, the test that covers it, and what the author must supply (a meaningful title or `labelledBy`, series names, units, the page's own structure). It also states the limits in section 3.8. | Published with each release; a CI check that every listed test exists | R11 |\n| A10 | Every built-in string can be replaced through `messages`. Built-in English text is marked `lang=\"en\"` when the document language is not English. | Test on a `lang=\"de\"` page | WCAG 3.1.2 |\n\n### 3.5 Diagnostic registry\n\nLevel decides delivery. **Error**: the chart or a series cannot be drawn; the chart shows its error state. **Warning**: something is not drawn as given; the reader sees the note in F12. **Hint**: advice for the author. Errors and warnings print once per code per page in every build; hints print in development builds only. `strict: true` throws on errors and warnings found while validating a create, `update` or `append` call, synchronously from that call. A diagnostic that can only arise later (a contrast check after the page's CSS changes, a custom formatter that throws when a table page is built, a label that stops fitting after a resize) is reported when it is found and is never thrown. Every diagnostic names the chart; those about data name the field and the first row indexes in the caller's own data.\n\n| Code | Level | Condition | What is drawn |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| AX100 | Error | Typed columns have different lengths | Error state; the message names the columns and their lengths |\n| AX101 | Error | A named field is not a key of any row | Error state; the message lists the keys that exist and the closest match |\n| AX102 | Warning | A field is absent (`undefined`) in some rows | Gaps |\n| AX103 | Warning | A value is `NaN` or infinite | Gap |\n| AX104 | Hint | Every non-missing value of a value field is a plain decimal string (`'40'`, `'-1.5'`) | Parsed and drawn; the message suggests `parse: 'number'`. An empty string counts as missing |\n| AX105 | Hint | Steps are absent on a regular time, date, wall-clock or number axis | Gaps. When a series would need more absent steps than twice its observations (and more than 1,000), it is treated as irregular instead: no gaps are created and the hint says so |\n| AX106 | Warning | A value field mixes numbers with strings, or holds strings that are not plain decimals (`'1,234'`, `'n/a'`) | The strings are not drawn |\n| AX107 | Warning | A date or time cannot be parsed | That row is not drawn |\n| AX108 | Error | Duplicate x within a series, or duplicate donut labels | That series, or the donut, is not drawn |\n| AX109 | Hint | Numbers on an axis declared `time` look like seconds, or a numeric x looks like epoch milliseconds without `type: 'time'` | As declared |\n| AX110 | Warning | An author domain on bar or area excluded zero | Domain widened to zero |\n| AX111 | Hint | Instants look like calendar dates | As instants, zone shown |\n| AX112 | Hint | Date-time strings have no offset | As written |\n| AX120 | Error | Negative donut value | Error state |\n| AX121 | Warning | More than eight donut slices | Seven largest plus \"Other (n)\" |\n| AX130 | Warning | More than eight series | All series, with dash or marker differences |\n| AX140 | Warning | No `title` and no `labelledBy` | Generated name |\n| AX141 | Warning | Palette below 3:1 on the resolved background | As themed |\n| AX142 | Hint | Plot too small to draw | Summary and table control |\n| AX143 | Hint | Labels hidden after every remedy | Note shown to the reader |\n| AX150 | Warning | Unknown option key | Ignored; the message suggests the closest known key |\n| AX151 | Warning | `append` rows out of order | Accepted through the slower full update |\n| AX131 | Hint | A compact chart has more than one series | Drawn, with dash differences |\n| AX132 | Hint | More bars than pixels along the category axis | Drawn; the message suggests aggregating |\n| AX133 | Hint | A scatter chart has more than 50,000 points | Drawn; the message says drawing may be slow |\n| AX134 | Error | Series × x positions exceeds 20,000,000 (long rows with many series and little shared x) | Error state; the message gives both counts and suggests fewer series or a scatter chart |\n| AX152 | Warning | `timeZone` is not a zone the browser knows | The viewer's zone is used |\n| AX160 | Warning | A custom formatter threw | The default format is used for that value |\n| AX199 | Error | Axylo itself failed while building the chart | Error state with a generic message; the error is passed to `onDiagnostic` and printed |\n\nExpected results for the Phase 1 fixtures: the combined `dirty` fixture (`[10, 20, null, NaN, '40', undefined, 70]`) yields AX102, AX103 and AX106 once each, draws Mon–Tue as a segment and Sun as a point, and shows the F12 note. `dirty-string` (one `'40'` among numbers) yields AX106. A column made only of numeric strings yields AX104 and is drawn in full. `dirty-null` yields nothing.\n\nThe split between AX104 and AX106 answers a reviewer's objection that common back ends return counts as strings (Postgres `bigint` and `numeric`, BigQuery): a whole column of plain decimal strings is unambiguous and is parsed; a stray string among numbers is a bug and is not.\n\n### 3.6 Type matrix\n\n| | Line | Area | Bar | Donut | Scatter |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Picking by pointer | Nearest x | Nearest x | The band under the pointer | The slice under the pointer | Nearest point within 24 px; ties go to the later row |\n| Tooltip content | Every visible series at that x | Same, plus the stack total | Every series in that category | Label, value, percentage | The point's series, x and y; coincident points listed, up to eight |\n| Keyboard order | By x, then series | By x, then series | By category, then series | By slice order | By x, then y |\n| Zoom | On time, date and numeric x only | On time, date and numeric x only | On time, date and numeric x only | No | Yes, both axes later; x only in 1.0 |\n| Simplified drawing when dense | F5 | F5 on cumulative sums | No; more bars than pixels raises a hint | No | No in 1.0; above 50,000 points a hint says drawing may be slow |\n| Limit that raises a diagnostic | Eight series | Eight series | Eight series | Eight slices | Eight series |\n| Reference lines and bands | Yes; included in the domain; listed in the summary and table caption | Yes | Yes | No | Yes |\n\n### 3.7 Compact variant\n\n`compact: true` on line, area and bar draws the marks and nothing else: no axes, gridlines, legend, toolbar, table control, zoom or tooltip. It is for a small tile where the page already shows the number and the label.\n\n| ID | Criterion | Verified by |\n|---|---|---|\n| C1 | A compact chart is a static image with a full text alternative: `role=\"img\"`, an accessible name (as in A1, including AX140 when none is given) and the generated summary as its description (series, x range, minimum, maximum, last value, omissions). It has no tab stop and no pointer-only behavior, so nothing is available to a mouse that is not available to a keyboard. | Accessibility-tree snapshot; a page of 40 has zero added tab stops |\n| C2 | The fidelity rules still hold: zero baseline for bar and area (F1), gaps (F2), diagnostics (F3), simplified drawing (F5), sorting (F11), no motion (I5), scheme from the page (L4). | The same fixtures as the full charts |\n| C3 | It fills its container at any size from 24×16 CSS px up; with no definite height it is 32 px tall. | Size tests |\n| C4 | With more than one series, lines differ by dash as well as color, since there is no legend; AX131 suggests one series per compact chart. | Two-series fixture |\n| C5 | When values were left out (F12), the description says so and a small marker with a text equivalent appears in the corner. | `dirty` fixture in compact mode |\n\nA compact chart does not give access to every value; the summary is its alternative. An author who needs every value reachable uses the full chart.\n\n### 3.8 What the evidence will and will not show\n\nAutomated checks establish structure: names, roles, focus order, contrast, text. They do not establish that a screen-reader user finds a chart understandable. The only evidence for that in 1.0 is A3's rehearsal by the developer on five reader and browser pairs, recorded as rehearsal. Axylo is built by one person with no access to study participants, so no claim of usability testing with disabled users is made, and the conformance table says so in those words. Reviewers proposed participant testing as a release gate; it is recorded here as a known limit instead.\n\n## 4. Acceptance criteria: the developer's experience\n\n| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| DX1 | The first chart takes ten lines or fewer, including import, by npm and by script tag. No required option besides data and fields. | Two CI tests that render the documented snippet from the packed distribution | D1 |\n| DX2 | One logical table, three ways to pass it: long rows, wide rows, typed columns. The three forms of each fixture produce the same normalized data and the same diagnostics, with row indexes in the caller's own form. | Equivalence tests | D2 |\n| DX3 | With typed rows, a misspelled field, a text field on a value axis, wide `y` combined with `series`, and an option that does not apply to the chart type all fail to compile. At run time, for untyped rows, the same mistakes raise AX101, AX106 and AX150. | [docs/api/examples.ts](api/examples.ts) compiles and each marked mistake fails (already checked with TypeScript 7.0.2); runtime tests per code | D5 |\n| DX4 | Diagnostics follow the registry in section 3.5: stable code, chart name, field and rows where they apply, the fix, and a documentation page per code. | Message snapshots; link checker | R5, D5 |\n| DX5 | Three methods change a chart: `update` changes options, `append` adds rows, `zoom` sets the view. They apply in call order. Validation and diagnostics are synchronous; drawing is deferred to the next frame; marks, labels, hit testing and the accessibility layer always switch to a new state together. `destroy()` releases everything and cancels pending work. No `resize()` exists. | Sequence tests: replace then append then zoom in one turn; a rejected `strict` update leaves the last valid chart; destroy before the frame; 1,000 create–destroy cycles show no heap growth trend (P7) | D3, R16 |\n| DX6 | Colors, font and spacing are CSS custom properties with documented names; the chart inherits `font-family` and `color`. `colors` pins a color to a series name so the same series has the same color on every chart. There is no theme object and no positional palette option. | Theming tests, including a 24-tile page where series subsets differ | R10 |\n| DX7 | Zero runtime dependencies (reconsidered only with a measured comparison). Size targets, set by the owner on 2026-11-18 after measurement: 96 KiB gzipped for everything and 80 KiB for a page that uses only `line`, CSS included. (The first targets, 40 and 25 KiB, were written before anything was built; an analysis of the built library showed they cannot hold the accessibility layer, the table, diagnostics, three kinds of time and streaming: `reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/size.analysis.md`.) | size-limit in CI on the packed distribution | D4 |\n| DX8 | Importing in Node without a DOM does not throw, and `validate()` runs there. Creating, destroying and creating again on the same element works and leaks nothing (the sequence a framework's strict or development mode produces). Works under a Content Security Policy without `unsafe-inline` or `unsafe-eval`, served with real headers. Labels and formatter output are inserted as text, never as HTML. A target that matches no element throws an error that names the selector. | CI tests | D1 |\n| DX9 | Semantic versioning. A deprecated API keeps working for at least one minor release and six months. At most one major version a year. The previous release's documented examples still compile and run against each release candidate. | Release policy; API report diff; upgrade test | D6 |\n| DX10 | Every runnable example in the documentation is compiled and rendered in CI from the packed distribution. | Docs build | D1 |\n| DX11 | MIT license for everything, including zoom and accessibility. Third-party notices are verified at release. | Repository; release check | D7 |\n| DX12 | A coding agent gets the first chart right. Given fixed prompts and trap fixtures (UTC-midnight ISO dates, numeric strings, unsorted rows, missing days, a misspelled field), an agent with only the published docs produces a chart with no error diagnostics that passes the reader criteria. Recorded as agent rehearsal, not as user evidence. | Scripted rehearsal at each release | D1, 1.2 |\n\n## 5. Performance budgets\n\nThese numbers are provisional. They are design targets taken from Phase 1's order-of-magnitude measurements, and they become release gates only after the benchmark harness built in M0 has measured a working Axylo slice in M1 and the limits have been recalibrated and frozen (plan, M1 and M6). Each budget names its workload, metric and statistic so that two people measuring the same build get the same verdict.\n\n| ID | Workload | Metric | Statistic | Provisional limit | Phase 1 reference |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| P1 | `trend` (3×120), default options, reference desktop | Main-thread task time from the call until the chart is complete | Median of 20 runs | 10 ms | Not comparable across libraries below about 35 ms with the Phase 1 method |\n| P2 | One series, 100,000 and 1,000,000 points, typed columns | Same | Median of 20 | 60 ms and 250 ms | See 01, section 5.3: uPlot and tuned Chart.js are the fastest measured |\n| P3 | 600-point window, one appended point per display frame at 60 Hz, driven by an independent clock | Main-thread task time per update (per run: task time in the window divided by the number of updates); and frames later than 25 ms | Median over runs; count per 300 frames | 2 ms; 0 | See 01, section 5.3 |\n| P4 | Pointer moved across a 100,000-point chart | Time from input to the frame showing the matching readout | 95th percentile | One frame (16.7 ms at 60 Hz) | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n| P5 | 24 `trend` charts created on one page, each tile 340 × 200 px | Total main-thread task time | Median of 20 | 200 ms | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n| P6 | Download | Minified and gzipped bytes, CSS included | Exact | DX7 | 01, section 1.4 |\n| P8 | 40 compact charts created on one page, each 160 × 48 px with one series | Total main-thread task time | Median of 20 | 80 ms | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n| P7 | Memory | Bytes owned by Axylo beyond the caller's columns for 1,000,000 points; heap trend over 1,000 create–destroy cycles | Measured after forced collection | At most 1× the size of the caller's two columns; no upward slope | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n\nThree rules hold whatever the numbers become:\n\n### 1.5 Handle and commit contract\n\n```ts\nchart.update({ title: 'Sessions' });                 // options\nchart.append([{ day: '2026-05-01', channel: 'Direct', sessions: 41210 }], { keep: 120 });   // rows\nchart.zoom(['2026-03-01', '2026-03-31']);            // view\nconst off = chart.on('hover', point => { … });\nchart.diagnostics;                                   // current, as soon as any call returns\nchart.destroy();\n```\n\nFinding D3: the ten libraries have seven update idioms. Axylo has three methods, one per kind of change, with one contract.\n\n| Rule | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| Order | Calls are applied in the order made. `update({ data })` followed by `append` appends to the new data. |\n| Two phases | Each call validates and normalizes synchronously: diagnostics are current when it returns, and a `strict` chart throws from that call. Drawing is deferred to the next animation frame, and calls made before that frame are drawn once. |\n| Atomic switch | Marks, labels, hit testing and the accessibility layer move to a new data revision together. No frame mixes two revisions. |\n| Patches | A key present in an `update` replaces the old value whole; a key set to `undefined` returns to its default; absent keys stay. No deep merging. |\n| Rejected changes | A call that throws under `strict` leaves the chart on its last valid state. |\n| Streaming | `keep` is the number of distinct x values retained. Rows before the last x are accepted through the slower full update and raise AX151. While zoomed to the latest edge the view follows new data; zoomed elsewhere, it stays put. Paused charts buffer (I6). Keyboard focus stays on its datum until that datum leaves the window (A7). |\n| Teardown | `destroy()` cancels the pending frame, disconnects observers and listeners, and makes later calls no-ops. |\n\n## 2. Architecture\n\n### 2.1 Pipeline\n\n```\noptions ─► normalize ─► frame ──┬─► view model ─► layout ─► scene ─► mark renderer\n            │ validate   (authoritative) │   scales, visible range         text renderer (DOM)\n            ▼ diagnostics                ├─► lookup: exact values by x, by point, by ordinal\n                                         ├─► summary and table\n                                         └─► focus model\n```\n\nThe **frame** is the normalized, lossless data: typed columns, a validity bitmap, dictionary-encoded categories, source-row indexes and a revision number. It is the only source for exact values. The table, the summary, keyboard navigation, tooltips and events read the frame. The **scene** is geometry for drawing and may be simplified (F5); nothing reads values back from it. The first draft derived accessibility from the scene, and two reviewers showed that a simplified drawing cannot return the samples it dropped.\n\n| Stage | Output | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| normalize | Frame, diagnostics | The only place raw input is read. Never coerces silently. |\n| view model | Scales, visible index ranges, series visibility | Domain rules implement F1 and F7. |\n| lookup | Nearest-x by binary search; nearest-point by a grid index (scatter) | Works on the frame within the visible range; cost bounded independently of the drawing. |\n| layout | Plot rectangle, tick sets, label boxes, legend and control placement | Implements L1, L3, L8. Takes a text-measurement interface as input. |\n| scene | Flat list of primitives (`path`, `rect`, `arc`, `rule`, `text`) | Plain data; testable in Node with injected text metrics. |\n| renderers | Marks; DOM text; accessibility DOM | See 2.3. |\n\n### 2.2 Invalidation\n\nStages are cached, and the cache keys are explicit. Caching on object identity alone, as the first draft said, would miss font loads, pixel-ratio changes and in-place mutation.\n\n| Stage | Reruns when |\n|---|---|\n| normalize | Data revision or field encoding changes. `append` extends the frame without renormalizing earlier rows. |\n| view model | Frame revision, zoom, series visibility, author domains. |\n| layout | View model; container size; resolved font and text spacing; `document.fonts` finishing a load; locale; any label text. A data-only update reruns layout when the tick labels would change, and skips it otherwise. |\n| scene | Layout or view model. |\n| mark raster | Scene; device pixel ratio; resolved colors (L4's triggers). |\n\nText is measured with the page's computed font, letter spacing and word spacing applied to the measuring context, and layout reruns when fonts finish loading. The measurement interface is a parameter, so Node tests inject fixed metrics and browser tests use the real one.\n\n### 2.3 Rendering\n\n**Working assumption: Canvas 2D for marks, HTML for all text.** Text stays in the DOM so that it inherits the page font and color, honors user text spacing and zoom (A6), adapts in forced-colors mode, and can be selected and translated.\n\n**This is reopened and decided in M1 by measurement.** The first draft justified canvas by Phase 1's slow SVG libraries, but those create a node per point; the one-path-per-series SVG chart in the harness was fast. With drawing simplified to a few vertices per pixel column (F5), an SVG path per series is small. SVG follows CSS variables and class-toggled themes with no repaint code, adapts to forced colors, prints sharply, needs no pixel-ratio handling and allocates no backing store (a 1,920×1,080 canvas at pixel ratio 2 is about 33 MB). Canvas is likely cheaper for per-frame redraw when streaming and hovering.\n\nM1 builds both mark renderers behind the same scene and compares them on: P1, P2, P3, P5, bytes, memory, a runtime theme toggle, forced colors, print, and browser zoom. The scene is renderer-neutral either way, so the choice is contained.\n\n**Simplified drawing.** Per device-pixel column and per contiguous run of valid samples, the stroke covers the run's minimum and maximum (F5). One rule for when it applies: more than four visible samples per device-pixel column on average. The pass over visible samples is O(visible) on typed columns and is measured separately from drawing; the vertex count is bounded by width times the number of runs, not by width alone.\n\n**No animation system.** First render is not animated (I5). Updates cut, or tween for at most 200 ms where the cost is negligible, and not at all under reduced motion.\n\n### 2.4 Document structure\n\n```html\n<figure class=\"axylo\" id=\"ax7\" role=\"group\" aria-labelledby=\"ax7-t\" aria-describedby=\"ax7-s\">\n  <figcaption>\n    <h3 id=\"ax7-t\">Daily sessions by channel</h3>        <!-- level from options; or omitted when labelledBy is used -->\n    <p id=\"ax7-s\" class=\"axylo-summary\">Line chart, 3 series, …</p>\n  </figcaption>\n  <div class=\"axylo-plot\" tabindex=\"0\"> … marks … <div class=\"axylo-text\" aria-hidden=\"true\">tick labels</div></div>\n  <div class=\"axylo-toolbar\" role=\"toolbar\" aria-label=\"Chart controls\">   <!-- one tab stop, roving focus -->\n    legend toggles · zoom in · zoom out · reset · pause · view as table\n  </div>\n  <p class=\"axylo-note\" role=\"status\">Values not shown: 2. See table.</p>\n  <div class=\"axylo-live\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div>\n  <div class=\"axylo-tooltip\" hidden></div>\n  <div class=\"axylo-table\" hidden><!-- built when opened: caption, headers, pager --></div>\n</figure>\n```\n\nIds are generated per chart. The plot's role, and how a focused point is conveyed, are not fixed here: M1 runs a spike with three candidate patterns and M4 confirms the choice (section 4).\n\nDefault styles are applied without injecting a `<style>` element, and a standalone stylesheet ships for pages that prefer to load it themselves. Which mechanism passes a strict Content Security Policy is settled by the M7 test with real headers, not assumed.\n\n### 2.5 Repository\n\n```\npackages/axylo/        core library (src/, test/)\n(packages/react/      @axylo/react: removed from 1.0, see 1.7)\napps/docs/             documentation site; runnable examples are test fixtures\nbench/                 benchmark harness (04-plan-performance.md), built in M0\nacceptance/            the acceptance matrix and the fixtures it uses\nresearch/              Phase 1 evidence and the ten-library harness\ndocs/api/              the type-checked API sketch, replaced by generated declarations in M0\n```\n\nTooling: TypeScript strict; ESM output with `.d.ts`, plus IIFE files for `<script>` use; Vitest; fast-check for property tests; Playwright on Chromium, Firefox and WebKit; axe-core; size-limit on the packed distribution; API Extractor so that public API changes fail CI until reviewed; Changesets.\n\nCI runs on Linux, Windows and macOS. Windows runs the browser suite natively on Chromium, Firefox and Edge. Playwright's WebKit build stands in for Safari in CI; real Safari on macOS and iOS is a manual release check, named separately in results.\n\n## 3. Milestones\n# Decision 0004: where the tooltip goes, and when the focused point's text changes\n\nStatus: decided by the coordinator on 2026-11-18 after the second review of the interaction layer (`reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core3.interaction.review.md`, new findings 3 and 4). Not rehearsed with a screen reader or a screen magnifier; both parts need that before they are called verified.\n\n## 1. The tooltip is anchored to the datum and never takes the pointer (revision 2)\n\nRevision 1 (placed across the reading axis, offset from the pointer, interactive while hovered) was implemented three times and still failed the acceptance tests: because the tooltip's position followed the pointer it moved away when approached, because it took pointer events it stalled the readout or swallowed the click whenever a move landed on it, and it could cover controls and the focus ring. Revision 2 removes the causes instead of tuning distances.\n\n**Problem.** WCAG 1.4.13 requires content shown on hover to stay when the pointer moves onto it. Spec I1 requires the readout to name the datum at the pointer on every move, the tooltip not to cover the active mark, to stay inside the viewport, and to fall back to a fixed readout when it cannot fit. On a line chart the whole plot picks, so anything that takes pointer events inside the plot blocks picking.\n\n**Rules.**\n\n1. **Position depends on the datum only.** For the datum on show, the tooltip is placed against the bounding box of that datum's active marks (all series at that x for line, area and bar; the point for scatter; the slice for donut), enlarged by the focus ring's width. The pointer's own position never enters the computation, so the tooltip does not move while the datum is unchanged, and it cannot run away from an approaching pointer.\n2. **Candidates, in order:** above the box, below it, then to the right and to the left of it (for horizontal bars: right, left, above, below). Along the other axis it is centred on the box and shifted only as far as needed. A candidate is valid when the tooltip lies wholly inside the plot rectangle, inside the viewport and inside every clipping ancestor, and overlaps neither the box nor any control. The first valid candidate is used. For a donut the first candidate is the hole, when the tooltip fits inside it.\n3. **A hovered tooltip never takes pointer events.** It is `pointer-events: none`. The plot keeps receiving every move, so picking never stalls at any speed, and a click goes to the plot and selects the datum under the pointer. Moving the pointer onto the tooltip therefore cannot hide it: on line, area and bar the tooltip stays for as long as the pointer's position along the reading axis still picks the same datum, which a straight approach across the reading axis does; on scatter and donut, while the pointer is inside the tooltip's rectangle the datum is held (computed from coordinates in the plot's own move handler, not by letting the tooltip take the event).\n3b. **A rest holds the datum along the way to the tooltip.** Rule 3 is enough when the tooltip sits across the reading axis (above or below), because a straight approach does not change what is picked. It is not enough when the straight path from the datum to its tooltip crosses positions that pick something else: a tooltip beside a tall bar or stack, a donut's tooltip in the hole or outside the ring, a scatter point's tooltip. For those: once the pointer has rested on a datum for 250 ms (no move larger than 2 CSS px), that datum is held while the pointer stays inside the corridor between its resting position and the tooltip's rectangle (the convex hull of the point and the rectangle) and inside the rectangle itself. Leaving the corridor, or leaving the rectangle on any side but back into the corridor, resumes picking at once. A pointer that has not rested is never held, so a sweep across the plot never stalls, whatever its speed. Placements across the reading axis never use the corridor.\n4. **Rows are not controls while hovered.** A hovered tooltip is text. Clicking or tapping the plot pins the tooltip for that datum; a pinned tooltip takes pointer events, stays until Escape, a click elsewhere, or its datum leaves the displayed frame, and its rows are buttons of at least 24 × 24 CSS px (WCAG 2.5.8) that select a series. Keyboard focus shows the same tooltip by the same placement; it is never over the focus ring because the box includes the ring.\n5. **Readout mode is decided by layout, not by hover.** When the plot is narrower than 240 CSS px or shorter than 140 CSS px, the figure is in readout mode: a strip of two text lines is reserved under the plot in the layout, and the content that would be in the tooltip is written there as running text (\"x: series value; series value; +N more\"), wrapping to the two lines and ending in \"+N more\" when it does not fit. The strip is part of the figure's flow: it covers nothing and nothing covers it. In readout mode there is no floating tooltip. A pinned datum makes the strip's entries buttons of 24 px height.\n6. **When no candidate is valid outside readout mode** (a tooltip taller than the room around a tall stack, for example), rows are dropped from the end and counted in \"+N more\" until a candidate is valid; if none is with one row, the tooltip is placed inside the plot at the candidate that overlaps the box least. This is the only case in which the active mark may be partly covered, and the table still has every value.\n7. Escape dismisses a hovered or pinned tooltip without moving the pointer, until the pointer reaches a different datum.\n\nRejected: revision 1; an interactive hovered tooltip with a \"safe triangle\" (still ambiguous when the reading axis and the approach coincide); always using the readout (loses the association between the values and the mark on plots that have room).\n\nWhat \"can itself be hovered\" (spec I1) means under this decision, for the tests: the pointer can be moved straight from the datum onto the tooltip's rectangle, and the tooltip is still shown, for the same datum, when the pointer is inside it. It does not mean the tooltip is the hit-test target.\n\n## 2. The focused point's text changes only for a reason the reader would name\n\n**Problem.** The plot's keyboard proxy is a native range whose value text describes the focused point, ending with its position (\"Point 4 of 120\"). While data streams in, the count changes on every append, so the text of a focused control changes without the reader acting. Spec A7 forbids unrequested announcements; whether a given screen reader speaks such a change cannot be tested here.\n\n**Rule.** The range's bounds and numeric value are always kept true to the displayed frame, silently. Its value text is rewritten only when:\n\n1. the reader moves (a key, an assistive adjustment, a pointer or touch selection that takes focus),\n2. the focused datum's own values change (the same x and series now read differently),\n3. the focused datum leaves the frame (the text must then say where focus went), or\n4. the plot regains focus.\n\nAn append that only changes the count or the ordinal of an unchanged datum does not rewrite the text. The position in the text can therefore be out of date until the next of the four events; it is recomputed then.\n\nTo check with a screen reader when one is available (added to decision 0001's list): idle appends with the plot focused are silent; an eviction of the focused point is spoken once; an increment immediately after an append lands on the expected neighbour and reads the current count.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'git show a518cbb:reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core3.fix.report.md\ngit show a518cbb:reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core4.fix.report.md\ngit show a518cbb:reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core5.fix.report.md\ngit show a518cbb:reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core6.fix.report.md\ngit show a518cbb:reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core7.fix.report.md\ngit show a518cbb:reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/tree-shake.report.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'git show a518cbb:reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core3.fix.report.md\ngit show a518cbb:reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core4.fix.report.md\ngit show a518cbb:reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core5.fix.report.md\ngit show a518cbb:reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core6.fix.report.md\ngit show a518cbb:reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core7.fix.report.md\ngit show a518cbb:reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/tree-shake.report.md'","aggregated_output":"<!-- Author: Claude Opus 5.5 (requested effort high), Agent-tool subagent a1d73b9f46c46814f, resumed. Saved by the coordinator from the hand-back. -->\n\n## Third fix pass: chart core (two third-round reviews, plus the later additions)\n\nPartly done. Both page-level blockers and all four controller blockers are fixed, the displayed snapshot is in place, and most controller majors are fixed. Several interaction majors and test-strength items are not done (listed below). Nothing is committed; the tree type-checks and `npm run build` works.\n\n### The snapshot design (`core/controller.ts`)\nI did not write the invariants as a block at the top of `controller.ts`; they live in the comments on `release()`, `showing()` and `frame()`. The property test checks them.\n\n- **Displayed snapshot.** `onScreen` holds the frame on screen, its store and, if needed, a copy of its rows. `drawn` holds the view, layout, lookup and reader for it. It changes only when a frame publishes, and becomes empty after a failed frame or a frame with no size.\n- **Row ownership.** `release()` runs after every accepted call and every published frame, whether or not frames run.\n  - It keeps the revision on screen and any accepted frame not yet drawn.\n  - The store can only retire \"everything up to revision N\". So once it grows past twice its last size, the frame on screen copies the rows it uses into a map, and everything older than the newest revision is retired.\n  - Result: `keep` bounds memory with zero frames or with every frame failing. This relies only on the data module's \"Frame lifetime\" rule (`rowAt` works until `retire()` reaches that revision).\n- **One generation per frame.** Author code runs, and the lazy `reader.summary`/`note` are materialized, before the first host write. If the chart changed or was destroyed by then, the frame stops having written nothing. Once writing has started, the frame finishes for the same state, and a newer call is drawn by the next frame. A destroyed controller swaps in an inert host.\n- **Error revision.** Each step runs on its own, in this order: interaction off, then the error message, then a new host `surfaces(false)` hides marks and text without calling their draw methods, then the content is cleared. The next good frame shows the surfaces again.\n- **Held state.** While paused, `diagnostics` include the held rows' findings, but the error state is decided only from what is on screen. A held frame is dropped only once its replacement is accepted, so a failed Resume keeps it and the next accepted call shows it.\n- **Notifications.** `onDiagnostic`, console output and events go through one in-order queue. Each delivery is fixed when it is made and delivered in full; only destruction stops it.\n\nThe fast-check property test (300 random sequences of append, title, data, pause, resume, zoom, frame, failing frame, heal) checks after every step: describe and lookup agree, every x on screen hovers to the caller's own row object, the prepared and committed views are the same, and retained rows ≤ keep + rows shown + 69.\n\n### Controller review\n\"Reproduced\" = the new test failed when run against 4809516 (`test/core/snapshot.test.ts` in a copy of that tree, browser tests against its bundle).\n\n| # | Reproduced | Fixed | Test |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| B1 superseded frame writes | yes | yes | snapshot \"blocker 1\" ×2 |\n| B2 Resume loses rows | yes | yes | snapshot \"blocker 2\" ×2 |\n| B3 renderer exception | yes | yes | snapshot \"blocker 3\" ×2 |\n| B4 keep unbounded | yes | yes | snapshot \"blocker 4\" ×3, property test |\n| 5 re-encode while paused / failed Resume | yes (failed Resume) | failed Resume: yes. Re-encode while paused: no | snapshot \"finding 5\" |\n| 6 append writes wrong state | yes | yes | snapshot \"finding 6\" ×3 |\n| 7 re-entrant deliveries | yes | yes | snapshot \"finding 7\" ×2 |\n| 8 stale AX101 after a valid append | not tested | no | — |\n| 9 late setup failure / kept unsubscribe | yes (unsubscribe) | yes (controller destroyed on late failure; per-subscription cleanup in `watchEnvironment`) | snapshot \"finding 9\" |\n| 10 two charts on one target | yes | yes (theme properties and scheme on the chart's own figure) | review3 \"finding 10\" |\n| 11 stream copies / scans | not tested | partly: formatters no longer hold the table. Not done: statistics cached by view, title update without a row-index allocation | — |\n| 12 resubmitted mutated objects | not tested | no | — |\n| 13 AX160 lifecycle | not tested | no | — |\n| 14 create exception → AX199 | yes | yes (delivered before anything is accepted; `validate` classifies the same way; undefined keys handled alike) | snapshot \"finding 14\" |\n| 15 scatter percent/ratio domain | yes | yes (`core/stages.ts`) | snapshot \"finding 15\" |\n| 16 follow at the latest datum | yes | yes (latest data x, `missingX` included) | snapshot \"finding 16\" ×2, missingX test |\n| 17 category zoom | — | no | — |\n| 18 all-missing bars | yes | yes | snapshot \"finding 18\" |\n| 19 environment changes | not run against 4809516 | yes: DPR and font epoch reread on reattach; `lang` watched; theme changes go through the frame scheduler (one draw per frame) | snapshot \"finding 19\" ×2 |\n| 20 weak tests | yes (host accessors) | partly: host keeps live accessors, records surfaces and writes after dispose; Node re-entrancy test rewritten. Other named tests not reworked | — |\n\n### Interaction review\n| Item | Reproduced | Fixed | Test |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| New B1 scatter value change hangs | yes (hung) | yes: global order, at most `count` steps | review3 \"blocker 1\" |\n| New B2 right-hand toolbar covered by the table | yes | yes: new plot column (`figure.stage`); table overlays only it; nothing frozen in pixels; focus moves into the table; pins close | review3 ×2 |\n| New 3 tooltip placement | — | partly: placed across the reading axis per decision 0004, keeps clear of pointer and mark, readout kept inside the viewport and clipping. Reserved readout space (rule 4) not done | interaction \"four plot edges\", acceptance I1 |\n| New 4 focused text | — | yes, decision 0004 part 2: text rewritten only for a move, a changed datum, a datum that left, or regained focus | review2 B4 test (changed to match the decision) |\n| New 5 data replacement and focus | not tested | yes | none dedicated |\n| Part 1 #7 coincident count | — | yes: full count kept, focused point listed first | — |\n| Part 1 #8 single-pointer pan | — | yes: Pan left / Pan right in the toolbar while zoomed, unavailable at an end | axe forced-colors list |\n| Part 1 #18 Jump to | — | yes: parsed per locale with a unit; malformed grouping rejected; categories exact; visible `jumpNotFound` error with an example | review2 jump test |\n| Part 1 #19 language | — | partly: summary, plot label and role marked `lang=\"en\"` | acceptance A10 (passes) |\n| Instructions | — | yes: `plotInstructions` is the plot's description | — |\n\nNot done:\n- New items 6–10 (too-small/compact focus handoff and overlay reset; Zoom in that can zoom out; misleading slider in loading and all-hidden states; touch-sized row actions; cancelled pinch frame).\n- Part 1 items 2 (exact identity for pins and dismissal), 3 (slice list availability), 9 (attainable minimum), 10, 11, 12 (CSS transforms), 13, 15 (shrinking an open table: one test passes, nothing more done).\n- Part 3, the list of weak tests.\n\n**Edit you asked for:** the dark-toggle test now compares rendered pixels with each scheme's colour as resolved in the page, and first asserts the two colours differ.\n\n### Acceptance-suite requests\n- **F4 scatter month/year precision:** fixed (precision read from the caller's rows; the point frame has no field for it).\n- **F7 domain ends at a drawn tick:** fixed in `core/axes.ts`, not in scale or layout. Layout may only choose tick sets whose first and last ticks are the domain's ends with no tick between an extreme and its end. Property test `test/core/ticks.test.ts` covers line, bar and scatter for container heights 300–700 px.\n- **Below about 200 px of plot F7 can still fail:** five-interval rounding needs more ticks than fit. Fixing that needs the domain chosen in layout with the data extent, a scale/layout change.\n- **A4 x unit in the table header:** wired; passes.\n- **L3 zero-size:** passes (no draw call before anything was drawn).\n- **A10, A5 bar gap, I1 bar, I1 scatter:** pass.\n- **L8:** the visible summary is now the first sentence; the full summary moved to a separate description element, still the figure's `aria-describedby`.\n- **Still failing:**\n  - **L1 at 200 % text, 240 px wide (line, area, scatter, donut):** the one-line summary falls outside the figure's height.\n  - **C4 compact bar:** the pattern is drawn, but the test reads cues from the series group element, and `render/svg.ts` (not mine) puts the pattern on the child rects.\n- **Messages I build myself:** \"Total\" (tooltip and value text), `pan*`, `jumpNotFound`, `plotInstructions`. None of the sentences `seeTable` affects. The donut slice text joins value and share as `${value} (${percent})` in `dom/interaction.ts`.\n\n### Edits outside `src/core` and `src/dom`\n| File | Change | Test |\n|---|---|---|\n| `src/view/index.ts` | `scatterX` includes `missingX` | `test/view/missing-x.test.ts` |\n| `src/scene/bars.ts` | grouped bars 1 px narrower (1 px of background between them); compact multi-series bars get the pattern cue | `test/scene/scene.test.ts`: two geometry expectations changed to the new widths, plus a gap check |\n| `src/render/canvas.ts` | bar edges snapped to device pixels | browser separator test, both renderers |\n| `src/theme/watch.ts` | watches `lang`; calls back synchronously per observer batch so the controller schedules its frame | existing theme tests; snapshot \"finding 19\" |\n| `src/theme/css.ts` (figure section) | stage, table overlay, readout, swatch dash, jump error, one-line summary | — |\n| `core/stages.ts`, `test/core/host.ts` | as granted | — |\n\nThe two changed expectations in `test/scene/scene.test.ts` belong to the scene module's author; they should review them.\n\n### Last lines of each run\n- **Type check:** `tsc -p packages/axylo` and `tsc -p docs/api` both clean.\n- **Node core:** `Tests 114 passed (114)`. Node acceptance: `Tests 494 passed (494)`.\n- **`npm test`:** `1 failed | 1209 passed`. The one failure is simplify's million-point timing test, not mine.\n- **Owned browser directories:** SVG `Tests 163 passed (163)`; canvas `Tests 163 passed (163)`.\n- **Browser acceptance:** SVG `Tests 5 failed | 452 passed (457)` (the five listed above).\n- **Browser acceptance with canvas:** `135 failed`. The suite reads the SVG DOM (`svg.axylo-svg`, `getScreenCTM`). My suite-wide canvas switch in `chart/util.ts` also reaches these tests, so they fail by construction, not because of the library. The suite needs an opt-out or a renderer-agnostic way of reading marks.\n\n### Not observable here\n- Screen-reader speech: decision 0004 part 2, and `aria-valuetext`, which Chrome's accessibility tree does not report (it gives the number).\n- Behaviour under CSS transforms.\n- Real GPU rasterization.\n\n### Contract changes needed\n- **`DatumRef` comment**, corrected sentence: \"x position (category: its index in the frame that produced the ref; categories can slide when a streaming window evicts them, so a holder remaps by category name across frames of one epoch).\"\n- **Bar cue past 32 series:** `RectMark.pattern` has only three kinds, so series 9 and 33 repeat colour and pattern. Spec F9 needs more pattern kinds in the contract.\n- **`plotInstructions` default:** the current default (\"Enter shows one series at a time\") misdescribes the keys. Proposed: \"Left and right arrows move between points, up and down between series, and Enter selects. Plus and minus zoom; 0 resets.\"\n\n<!-- Author: Claude Opus 5.5 (requested effort high), Agent-tool subagent a1d73b9f46c46814f, resumed. Saved by the coordinator from the hand-back. -->\n\n## Chart core, fourth fix pass: report\n\nNothing is committed. I stopped at an item boundary. The library type-checks for my files. The one remaining type-check error and the Node acceptance failures are in files that other agents were editing during my runs (see \"Runs\").\n\n### Your three corrections\n1. **The `lang` change no longer fires the theme watcher.** `src/theme/watch.ts` is back to `['class','style','data-theme']`. `lang` is now watched by its own observer, `watchLanguage` in `core/environment.ts`, wired in `core/chart.ts`. It calls `invalidate.language()`, which schedules a frame. `test/browser/render/theme.browser.test.ts` is unedited (`git diff` is empty) and passes 18/18. The language watcher has no browser test of its own yet.\n2. **C4 compact bar: what the group would need.** The test reads only elements whose inline `color` is `var(--axylo-series-color-N)`. In SVG that is the series `<g>`, which has fill `currentColor`. The pattern sits on the overlay child `<rect>`s, which carry no colour style. So both series read `none||` and come out equal.\n   - The cleanest fix in `src/render/svg.ts` `updateBars`: also set `style=\"color: var(--axylo-series-color-N)\"` on the overlay rects (the ones with `fill: url(#axylo-pattern-…)`). The second series then reads `none||pattern`, and nothing changes visually because the pattern's content takes its colour from the `<pattern>` element.\n   - Putting `fill=\"url(#…)\"` on the `<g>` would also pass, but it would be an attribute the CSS overrides, which misdescribes the drawing.\n   - The test's reading is reasonable but tied to SVG.\n3. **L1 at 200% text, 240 px.** The visible first sentence of the summary now wraps (`overflow-wrap: anywhere`, no `nowrap`). The L1 tests pass in the browser acceptance runs.\n\n### Controller review\n| # | Fixed | Test (failed before the fix; \"old\" means the d49e8a8 bundle or tree) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 5 | Re-encoding while paused redraws the displayed rows with the new fields and keeps held rows buffered until Resume | `test/core/snapshot.test.ts` \"pass 4: findings 5 and 8\" |\n| 8 | A valid append clears a stale AX101 (all five types) | same |\n| 12 | Small option objects are copied when accepted (borrowed `data` is not). A label- or unit-only change renames without renormalizing | `snapshot.test.ts` \"finding 12\"; `options.test.ts` |\n| 13 | The AX160 bucket is emptied before formatters are built (so construction-time AX160 survives) and kept while they live. Same code and field are deduplicated. The layout bucket is cleared when no axes are drawn | 4 tests \"finding 13\"; all 4 failed on the old controller |\n| 11 | A title update on a stream no longer walks the retained rows. Summary statistics are reused when only naming inputs change | 2 tests \"finding 11\"; both failed first. Formatter closures not capturing the table: checked by reading only. The buffer copy on append belongs to the data module |\n| 17 (rescoped) | `zoom()` on a category axis or a donut is a no-op reported as AX150 (`field: 'zoom'`). A window kept from a numeric axis never reaches categories | line, area, bar and donut tests \"finding 17\". My partial category-zoom code (band offset, layout offset, whole-category steps) was removed with `git checkout` of my own files |\n| F7 below 200 px | New `coarserDomains` in `scale/domain.ts`. Layout's `ValueAxis.coarser` makes `computeLayout` retry with the first wider domain whose tick set fits whole. Core supplies the candidates. The extent is cached per view and is in the layout key | `test/core/ticks.test.ts` heights 100–700 (failed at 192 px before); `test/scale/domain.test.ts`; `test/layout/layout.test.ts` |\n| 20 (weak tests) | Re-entrancy test now runs its nested update. Dense-line test checks the drawn path. Browser crossing test checks series-colour pixels. `plotRole` test uses an override. A teardown test counts listeners through the DevTools protocol. A late-setup-failure test asserts a frame was requested before the failure | `controller.test.ts`, `review.browser.test.ts`, `lifecycle.browser.test.ts` |\n\n### Documentation author's items\n1. **AX108 on the only series** now shows an error state whose message is the series-not-drawn sentence. The summary follows the state, and the table is offered.\n2. **Event values match what `zoom()` accepts.** `positionValue` returns:\n   - a Date for time axes;\n   - `'YYYY-MM-DD'` for a date at midnight, otherwise the number (a padded bar end; `zoom()` reads it as the same position);\n   - the shortest offset-free ISO string for wall-clock axes;\n   - the category name, otherwise the number.\n\n   I also fixed a bug this exposed: a wall-clock window on a wall-clock axis was rejected because its AX112 hint counted as an error. Hints no longer reject a window, and that AX112 is dropped for wall axes. Round-trip tests pass for all four axis kinds. Sentence for `public.ts` on the zoom event and `Point.x`: \"In the form `zoom()` accepts for this axis (a Date for instants, 'YYYY-MM-DD' for dates, an ISO date-time without offset for wall-clock times, the category, else the number), so it can be passed to another chart's `zoom()` unchanged.\"\n3. **Loading and error summaries are now the state text**, no longer \"No data to show.\".\n4. **Donut percentages: no change.** The legend, tooltip, table and summary all use `formatters.percent`, so the tooltip shows six digits too. Spec F6 gives readouts six significant fraction digits, and F8 gives shares no rule. If one fraction digit is wanted, F8 needs a sentence, and the change is in `src/format`.\n\n### Reader review, finding 5\nThe reader has no exported sentence builder yet, so there is a marked call site in `controller.ts`. The stand-in follows the reader's `{seeTable}` rule; its shape is `seriesNotDrawnSentence(messages: Messages, series: string, table: boolean): string`. The note language now counts `seeTable` when the note's templates contain it. `FigureModel.noteSegments: {text, lang}[]` exists and `figure.ts` renders each run as a `<span lang>`. For now the controller passes one segment; the reader's per-run segments plug into that call site. One case stays wrong until then: an overridden template containing the built-in \"See table.\" is not tagged. Tests are in `snapshot.test.ts` \"reader review finding 5\".\n\n### Interaction review\n| Item | Fixed | Test (`test/browser/chart/review4.browser.test.ts`; all failed on the old bundle except where noted) |\n|---|---|---|\n| new 7 | `zoomStep` makes zoom-in strictly narrower and zoom-out strictly wider for keys, wheel and buttons | browser test, plus `focus2.test.ts` property and the reviewer's [40,45] case |\n| new 8 | Zoom controls and recovery are only offered for a real empty window. The plot reads the state text (\"Loading chart.\"). With every series hidden the range is 0/\"No data to show.\" and the legend still recovers | 2 tests |\n| new 6 | The overlay is cleared before the frame is dropped. Input state is reset. Focus goes to the table control, or to the figure (`tabindex=-1`, no new tab stop) | 2 tests |\n| new 10 | A pinch is bound to its two pointer ids. Cancel, blur, detach and rebuild drop the queued frame; a normal lift applies the last move. Mouse presses are always captured | pinch-cancel test fails on old. The release-outside test passes on old as well, so it is a guard only |\n| part 1 #2 | Pins and dismissals map by exact category; keyboard focus still moves to the successor | 2 tests (append with `keep`) |\n| part 1 #10 | same as new 10 | |\n| part 3, `focus2.test.ts:68` | Now starts from a known representable narrowing and asserts that zooming in is exhausted | |\n\n**Not done:** new item 9 (touch-sized row and slice actions); part 1 items 3 (not re-checked), 9 (minimum window not changed beyond the direction rule), 11, 12, 13 with decision 0004 rule 4, and 15; part 3's other weak tests.\n\n### Edits outside `src/core` and `src/dom`\n- `src/theme/watch.ts`: attribute list reverted (no test needed; the render theme test covers it).\n- `src/scale/domain.ts`, `src/scale/index.ts`: `coarserDomains`, tested in `test/scale/domain.test.ts`.\n- `src/layout/index.ts`, `src/layout/labels.ts`: `ValueAxis.coarser`, `FittedLabels.partial`, and the retry. Tested in `test/layout/layout.test.ts`.\n  - The layout author edited these same files and the test file at the same time; their code sits in the working tree beside mine.\n  - Once, for about ten seconds, I wrote the HEAD versions of `index.ts` and `labels.ts` and then restored my copy. Their edits are visible on top of mine afterwards, but ask them to check nothing was lost.\n- `src/theme/css.ts` (figure section).\n- `test/core/host.ts`: new `pageLang` option. Someone else also added `timeZone`, `locale`, `textWidth` and `captionLineHeight` there, and changed `src/index.ts` imports to `./public.js`.\n- **Shared bundle:** to show tests fail on the old code, I swapped `dist` to the d49e8a8 bundle several times and rebuilt afterwards. Other agents' browser runs in that window may have used the old bundle.\n\n### How to run acceptance on canvas\nDon't. The suite reads the SVG DOM. My renderer switch in `test/browser/chart/util.ts` now applies only to tests under `test/browser/(chart|a11y)/`, so `AXYLO_RENDERER=canvas` no longer reaches acceptance. Canvas is covered by my two directories.\n\n### Runs (last lines)\n- **Type check:** exit 1. The one error is `packages/axylo/test/acceptance/dx-diagnostics.test.ts(180,9) TS2322`, a file the acceptance agent was editing. An earlier run also showed an error in `src/reader/table.ts`, also edited by another agent.\n- **`npm test`:** `Test Files 13 failed | 62 passed (75)`, `Tests 83 failed | 2122 passed (2205)`. Every failure is in `test/acceptance`: 8 of the 13 files are `.scratch/` mutation runs, and the rest are files being edited. The same f-domains, f-donut, f-format, dx-diagnostics, f-stacks and dx-controller tests also fail against the HEAD tree.\n- **Node core, scale and layout:** `Test Files 18 passed (18)`, `Tests 284 passed (284)`.\n- **Browser, my directories, SVG:** `Test Files 12 passed (12)`, `Tests 174 passed (174)`.\n- **Browser, my directories, canvas:** `Test Files 12 passed (12)`, `Tests 174 passed (174)`.\n- **Browser acceptance, SVG:** `Test Files 4 failed | 9 passed (13)`, `Tests 14 failed | 455 passed (469)`. That run overlapped my bundle swaps and the suite's own edits. On a stable bundle the failing set is 9 tests (A7 bar sliding window, A10 bar, A2 focus indicators ×5, C4 compact bar, T-tooltip-donut), and the same 9 fail against the HEAD bundle.\n  - One real regression of mine, I3 bar (date `positionValue` dropping a padded end), is fixed and the I3 tests pass 20/20.\n  - I did not re-run the full acceptance suite after the last reader-sentence changes; my own directories were re-run on that final build.\n\nLogs are in `/tmp/claude-1000/-home-canarit--claude-spares-spare-6ClT4i/23402a37-81bb-469c-a14a-8bf7f048d066/scratchpad/core-fix/` (`p7-svg.txt`, `p7-canvas.txt`, `p6-acc-svg.txt`, `p7-npmtest.txt`, `p7-tsc.txt`).\n\n### Contract changes\nNone needed.\n\n<!-- Author: Claude Opus 5.5 (requested effort high), Agent-tool subagent a1d73b9f46c46814f, resumed. Saved by the coordinator from the hand-back. -->\n\n## Chart core, fifth pass: report\n\nNothing is committed and the tree compiles. Everything you listed is done except interaction item 13 with decision 0004 rule 4 (the readout's reserved space) and part of the part-3 weak-test list; both are explained under \"Not done\". I did not touch the shared `dist` to test old code: the old revision (88a8b96) is built privately at `/tmp/claude-1000/-home-canarit--claude-spares-spare-6ClT4i/23402a37-81bb-469c-a14a-8bf7f048d066/scratchpad/core-fix/old5/`. A new switch in `test/browser/chart/util.ts`, `AXYLO_BUNDLE=<dir>`, loads that bundle into pages of my own suites only.\n\n### 1. F7: the domain ends at the first drawn tick at or beyond each extreme\n- **Cause:** a floating-point comparison in `core/axes.ts` `endingCandidates`. The check `b - step < hi` let the 0.1-step tick set through, because 0.2 − 0.1000…09 comes out just below 0.1.\n- **Fix:** each tick set is now checked on its own tick values. The first tick at or above the maximum must be the last tick, and the last tick at or below the minimum must be the first. `scale/domain.ts` was already exact.\n- **Tests:**\n  - `test/core/axes.test.ts`: the [-0.8, 0.2] case (failed before the fix) and a property test.\n  - `test/scale/domain.test.ts`: property \"an extreme equal to k × step ends the domain there\".\n  - `f-domains.test.ts`: 28/28 pass.\n\n### 2. Donut shares: at most one fraction digit\n- A new `shareText` in `format/number.ts` is what `formatters.percent` now uses. It rounds the exact decimal, so 0.1235 reads 12.4%.\n- Values on a percent or ratio scale keep their F6 readout digits.\n- Tests in `test/format/index.test.ts`: 17.2%, the German form, 12.4%, 33.3%, and a ratio value that stays 17.241379%.\n\n### C4: the series colour on the pattern overlay\n- `render/svg.ts` `updateBars`: the overlay rects that carry the pattern also carry the series colour style. The colour is part of the cache key, so a theme change updates it.\n- Test in `test/browser/render/review.browser.test.ts`: the elements carrying the series colour whose fill is a pattern. It failed before the fix and passes now; the render suite is 84 passed, 1 skipped.\n\n### 3. Interaction review\n| Item | What changed | Old bundle |\n|---|---|---|\n| new 9 | Choosable tooltip rows and every donut slice-list entry (zero and null slices included) are buttons named by their text, with `tabindex=-1`, so the closed chart still has two tab stops. Clicking a slice entry selects it; the press does not move focus. Opening the table by keyboard closes a pin (this already worked). | 2 of 3 tests fail on old; the pin test passes on old and stays as a guard |\n| part 1 item 3 | All-zero donut with `table: false` still lists its slices. Already worked. | passes on old (guard) |\n| part 1 item 9 | `minimumWidth` is now at most half the axis and at least one unit in the last place: two or three positions over [0,100] can be halved, and [1e16, 1e16+4] halves to a width of 2. | Node tests in `focus2.test.ts` failed before the fix |\n| part 1 item 11 | A pinned scatter point is dropped when its point has no drawn anchor. Otherwise the group is rebuilt by picking at that anchor, so new coincident points are counted. | both tests fail on old |\n| part 1 item 12 | `core/chart.ts` `measure()` now gives layout pixels rather than transformed viewport pixels. One conversion (`cssScale` in `tooltip.ts`) is used for picking, anchors, tooltip size and placement, and the readout. Ratios within 2% are treated as offsetWidth's integer rounding, not as a transform. | fails on old |\n| part 1 item 15 | An open table and its control stay inside the card when the card shrinks and text doubles. Already worked: no inline heights. | passes on old (guard) |\n| language watcher | Changing `lang` on the document changes number formatting to `1.234,5`, marks built-in text `lang=\"en\"`, and runs no theme stage. | passes on old (guard) |\n\nThe new tests are in `test/browser/chart/review5.browser.test.ts`.\n\n**24 px rows trade-off (decision for you):**\n- With 24 px rows everywhere, the hover tooltip grew about 12 px. At two plot edges it then no longer fit beside the mark, so the existing test \"at each of the four plot edges…\" fell back to the readout and failed.\n- What I did: rows are 24 px when the tooltip is pinned by a tap, in the fixed readout, and in the slice list. A mouse-hover tooltip keeps text-height rows, which are still buttons.\n- So WCAG 2.5.8 is met wherever rows are acted on by touch. It is not met for mouse clicks on hover rows. If you want 24 px rows there too, tell me and I will change the edge test's fixture instead.\n\n### 4. Tests that passed with the behaviour broken\n- Click test (`review2`): now asserts the floating tooltip's title and the exact selected x at each of the four positions.\n- Contrast dash test: no longer skips when every plotted SVG path is solid.\n- Donut legend: a second fixture where value and share differ, expecting \"3 (60%)\" and \"2 (40%)\".\n- Zoom exclusions: now covers category bar, line, area and donut, by buttons and the `+` key.\n- Coarse-double loop in `focus2` (pass 4).\n\nExpectations I changed because the requirement changed: \"two or three positions cannot be zoomed\" (focus2 and review2) became the attainable-minimum rule; \"the legend has no buttons\" became buttons outside the tab order; the note runs in `snapshot.test.ts` are now per run.\n\n### 5. Reader helper, note runs and AX143\n- The stand-in is gone; the call site imports `seriesNotDrawnSentence` from `../reader`.\n- `FigureModel.noteSegments` takes the reader's runs. A run is marked `en` only on a page in another language.\n- Node test: overriding only `seeTable` leaves \"Siehe Tabelle.\" unmarked; overriding only the sentence marks the built-in \"See table.\" `en`.\n- `figure.ts` no longer adds spaces between runs; the reader's runs carry their own.\n- AX143 now passes `chartType`, `xKind` (taken from the frame for scatter as well) and `horizontal` for bars. Node test: vertical category bars get the horizontal-bar hint, a category line does not.\n\n### 6. Mixed-sign totals\n`focus.ts` already takes the total text from the lookup's tooltip model and computes no net. A Node test checks a stack of +3 and −2: the tooltip total is \"3 and -2\" and the focused text contains \"Total 3 and -2\".\n\nNone of my tests depend on worker threads, so the switch to forked processes changes nothing for them.\n\n### Edits outside `src/core` and `src/dom`\n- `src/render/svg.ts`: the C4 change only.\n- `src/format/number.ts` and `src/format/index.ts`: share precision only.\n- `src/theme/css.ts`: the figure section (choice buttons).\n- Test files: `test/scale/domain.test.ts`, `test/format/index.test.ts`, `test/browser/render/review.browser.test.ts`.\n\n### Not done\n- **Item 13 with rule 4 (reserved space for the readout).** Reserving space when the floating tooltip might not fit costs a small tile's plot room on every chart; that is why I reverted it in pass 3. Placing the readout inside the plot column stops it covering the controls but drops \"reserved\". I need a decision: reserve space only below some figure width (which width?), or allow the readout over the plot.\n- **Part 3, remaining:**\n  - the 40-move sweep at several strides;\n  - a clipped fallback that passes `isVisible()`;\n  - the native-range and narrowed-scatter adjustments;\n  - selecting a secondary row by touch in the readout (the pinned-tooltip case is covered);\n  - the area-marker oracle;\n  - the speech-related tests (screen-reader output cannot be observed here).\n\n### Runs (last lines)\n- **Type check:** exit 0.\n- **`npx vitest run packages/axylo/test --exclude '**/acceptance/**'`:** `Test Files 2 failed | 43 passed (45)`, `Tests 2 failed | 768 passed (770)`.\n  - `data/streaming-storage.test.ts` \"bounds the retained typed-array backing…\": data module, under edit.\n  - `simplify.test.ts` \"million-slot outlier\": a timing limit (38 ms against 30) under load; it passes when run alone.\n- **Browser chart and a11y, SVG:** `Test Files 13 passed (13)`, `Tests 184 passed (184)`.\n- **Browser chart and a11y, canvas:** `Test Files 13 passed (13)`, `Tests 184 passed (184)`.\n- **Browser render:** `Test Files 5 passed | 1 skipped (6)`, `Tests 84 passed | 1 skipped (85)`.\n- **`npx vitest run packages/axylo/test/acceptance/f-domains.test.ts`:** `Test Files 1 passed (1)`, `Tests 28 passed (28)`.\n\nLogs are `p9-*.txt` in `/tmp/claude-1000/-home-canarit--claude-spares-spare-6ClT4i/23402a37-81bb-469c-a14a-8bf7f048d066/scratchpad/core-fix/`.\n\nNo contract changes needed.\n\n<!-- Author: Claude Opus 5.5 (requested effort high), Agent-tool subagent a1d73b9f46c46814f, resumed. Saved by the coordinator from the hand-back. -->\n\n## Chart core, sixth pass: report\n\nNothing is committed. The browser acceptance suite (SVG) is at 538 of 551, up from 899/37 failures across all suites at d8b4150. The 13 that still fail are three groups: a test-procedure limit, a conflict between the decision's geometry and the test path, and a browser timing limit. Each is explained below with a suggested way out. I did not swap the shared `dist`: old-code checks used the privately built 0e5aa29 bundle through my `AXYLO_BUNDLE` switch.\n\n### Decision 0004 revision 2, implemented as written\n- **`src/dom/tooltip.ts`, rewritten.**\n  - **Placement:** the position comes from the datum alone: the box of its active marks, enlarged by the ring's reach (`RING_REACH` = 9 px).\n  - **Candidates:** above, below, right, left; horizontal bars right, left, above, below; donut tries the hole first. A candidate is valid only wholly inside the plot rectangle (inset 4 px, clear of the plot outline), the viewport and every clipping ancestor, overlapping neither the box nor any control of the figure.\n  - **Rule 6:** rows are dropped into \"+N more\"; if nothing is valid with one row, the candidate that overlaps the box least is used, inside the plot.\n  - **Hovered vs pinned:** a hovered tooltip has `pointer-events: none` and text rows; a pinned one takes events and its rows are buttons of at least 24 × 24 px.\n  - **`strip()`:** running text \"x: series value; …; +N more\" fitted to two lines; entries become 24 px buttons when pinned.\n- **`src/dom/interaction.ts`.**\n  - **Pinning:** a click pins, as a tap already did. A pinned tooltip ignores moves until Escape, a click elsewhere, a keyboard move, or its datum leaving.\n  - **Holding on scatter and donut:** the datum is held while the pointer is inside the rectangle its tooltip had (computed in the plot's own move handler).\n  - **Readout mode:** a class on the figure; the strip and the axis area under the plot count as hoverable.\n- **`src/core/controller.ts`, readout mode by layout.** The plot rectangle without the strip, laid out in the plot's whole column (`room`, from `chart.ts` `measure()`), is under 240 × 140. One more condition: the plot must keep the L8 minimum of 80 px beside the strip.\n  - Without that condition, the 24 tiles of 340 × 200 in I5 fell into the too-small fallback and drew nothing.\n  - With it, L8 by height and I5 both pass.\n  - The decision does not depend on the strip, so it does not oscillate.\n- **Figure and CSS:** the readout element now sits in the plot's column under the plot (`figure.ts`). The strip is two reserved lines (`css.ts`, figure section).\n- **Rings are drawn inside the plot's box** (`core/focus.ts` `buildOverlay`), so the ring at the last point and at the top of a donut is painted. The A2 focus-indicator tests pass.\n\n### Other items\n- **A7 bar:** the category remap now remaps the text's own datum too, so a sliding window no longer rewrites it. New test fails on 0e5aa29 and passes now.\n- **JSON in the page:** the source was `note.dataset.segments = JSON.stringify(runs)` in `figure.ts`. That comparison is now kept in memory. The distribution browser run passes (30/30).\n- **L1 late font:** the cause was that `FontFace.load()` followed by `document.fonts.add()` never fires `loadingdone`. A font probe in the text layer is watched with a ResizeObserver; when it changes size, the fonts are invalidated and everything is laid out again (`chart.ts`, `css.ts`). The L1 late-font tests pass.\n- **L8 short summary:** first sentence, at most two lines, ellipsis beyond (line clamp in `css.ts`). L8 tests pass.\n- **L4:** theme changes are now resolved and written inside the observer's callback, with the canvas still repainted once through the scheduler. The class, style and data-theme triggers pass on all six kinds.\n- **Wiring from the scene and lookup author:**\n  - `buildLookup` gets the device pixel ratio, and on scatter it is in the lookup's cache key.\n  - The donut focus outline uses `donutGeometry`'s adjusted angles.\n  - T-pointer-scatter and T-dense-donut pass.\n- **Item 5:** the reader's `seriesNotDrawnSentence` and runs were wired in pass 5 and still are. **AX143** passes `chartType`, `xKind` and `horizontal`.\n- **Item 6:** `focus.ts` takes the total from the lookup's tooltip model and computes no net total.\n\n### Still failing (13), and why\n1. **I1 \"readout mode … 139 px high\", 5 types.** Entering readout mode reserves the strip, so the plot jumps from about 140 px straight to about 92 px; no plot height exists in between. The test's bisection (`plotTo`) stops on whichever side it probed last, and here it is the 140.07 px side. At 139 px the library is in readout mode. The width bracket passes. This needs a test change: for example, report the last readout-side height of the bisection.\n2. **I1 bar hover.** The fixture's bars reach within about 40 px of the plot top, so neither \"above\" nor \"below\" fits. The tooltip goes beside the box, and the straight path crosses into the next band at step 1 (\"rows changed\"). Under rule 3 only an approach across the reading axis keeps the datum.\n3. **I1 donut hover.** \"Hole first\" puts the tooltip in the hole. The straight path from the Travel slice crosses the Rent slice (steps 10–18) and the empty hole before entering the rectangle. The tooltip shown last on the way was Rent's, which is narrower, so at step 24 the pointer is outside Rent's rectangle and nothing is held. Rule 3 holds a datum only inside its tooltip's rectangle.\n4. **L4, 6 kinds: only the `prefers-color-scheme` part fails.** Probe: after `emulateMedia({colorScheme:'dark'})`, `matchMedia(...).matches` is already true in the next rAF, but Chrome dispatches the `change` event one frame later. No JavaScript can resolve the theme earlier. A fix for charts with `color-scheme: light dark` would be for theme resolution (`src/theme`, not mine) to write `light-dark(light, dark)` values, which the browser switches by itself. Otherwise the test would need to wait two frames.\n\nFor items 2 and 3 I need your decision: change the test path, or change the decision (for example, no \"hole first\" on donut, or a hold along the path).\n\n### Expectations of my own tests changed for revision 2\nThese now assert the revised rules:\n- **The tooltip's position:**\n  - \"four plot edges\" no longer requires the pointer to be outside the tooltip; it asserts the tooltip does not move while the datum is unchanged.\n  - The scale(2) test checks the tooltip stays inside the plot instead of centred on the bar.\n- **Pinning and rows:**\n  - A click pins; a hovered row is text, and a pinned row is the button that selects.\n  - The hover-event sequence is adjusted for click-pinning.\n  - The coincident-count test accepts rows plus \"+N more\" adding up to nine.\n- **Readout mode:**\n  - The narrow-tile and axe readout tests use a 220 px tile, since readout mode now starts below 240 px.\n  - The readout text may end in \"+N more\".\n\nThere is also a new L4 first-frame test in `test/browser/chart/theme.browser.test.ts`. It passes on old code too, so it is a guard only.\n\n### Edits outside `src/core` and `src/dom`\n- **`src/theme/css.ts`** (figure section only): tooltip, strip, choice buttons, font probe, summary line clamp.\n- **No layout changes.** I tried a `minimum` option in `src/layout` during the pass, then removed it, and `src/layout` and `test/layout` match HEAD. The final full run happened before that removal, with the option present but unused.\n\n### Runs (last lines)\n- **Type check:** exit 0.\n- **`npx vitest run packages/axylo/test --exclude '**/acceptance/**'`:** `Test Files 1 failed | 45 passed (46)`, `Tests 1 failed | 775 passed (776)`. The one failure is the kept memory test in `data/streaming-storage`.\n- **My browser directories, SVG:** `Test Files 13 passed (13)`, `Tests 187 passed (187)`.\n- **My browser directories, canvas:** `Test Files 13 passed (13)`, `Tests 187 passed (187)`.\n- **`AXYLO_BROWSER=1 npx vitest run packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance` (SVG):** `Test Files 2 failed | 11 passed (13)`, `Tests 13 failed | 538 passed (551)`.\n- **`AXYLO_BROWSER=1 npx vitest run --config tests/dist/vitest.config.ts`:** `Test Files 4 passed (4)`, `Tests 30 passed (30)`.\n\nLogs are `r1-*.txt` in `/tmp/claude-1000/-home-canarit--claude-spares-spare-6ClT4i/23402a37-81bb-469c-a14a-8bf7f048d066/scratchpad/core-fix/`.\n\n<!-- Author: Claude Opus 5.5 (requested effort high), Agent-tool subagent a1d73b9f46c46814f, resumed. Saved by the coordinator from the hand-back. -->\n\n## Chart core, seventh pass: report\n\nAll five items are done. Browser acceptance on SVG now passes in full, 555 of 555, and both items you asked me to confirm (I5 and L1 bar at 200% text) pass in it. Nothing is committed; I did not swap the shared `dist` (old-code checks used private builds of 4504ca4 via `AXYLO_BUNDLE`).\n\n### 1. Rule 3b: a rest holds the datum along the corridor (`src/dom/interaction.ts`)\n- **Rest:** a pointer that stays within 2 CSS px for 250 ms, measured with the events' own timestamps, has rested.\n- **Where it applies:** when the datum's floating tooltip is not across the reading axis (beside a bar or stack; always on scatter and donut).\n- **Hold:** after a rest, the datum is held while the pointer is inside the convex hull of the resting point and the tooltip's rectangle. Leaving the hull resumes picking on that same move; a pointer that never rested is never held. The tooltip stays pointer-transparent and does not move.\n- **One adjustment:** the corridor opens on the first move after the rest, even a move of 2 px or less. Without that, a datum resting exactly on a band edge (the acceptance bar probe) changed on the first 1 px step.\n- **Tests:** five browser tests in `test/browser/chart/review6.browser.test.ts`:\n  - bar after a rest;\n  - bar without a rest, where the datum changes;\n  - leaving the rectangle away from the corridor;\n  - a fast sweep after a rest elsewhere;\n  - donut with the tooltip in the hole.\n\n  On 4504ca4 the three that need the hold fail; the no-rest and sweep tests also pass on old code, so they only guard.\n\n### 2. L4 on a prefers-color-scheme change, fixed in CSS (`src/theme/`)\n- **Stylesheet (`css.ts`):** the `.axylo` private defaults are now `light-dark(light, dark)`, so they switch in the frame the media query does. Fixed sets remain under `[data-axylo-scheme=\"light\"]` and `[data-axylo-scheme=\"dark\"]`. The comment says `light-dark()` is supported by the current and previous major versions of the four browsers in spec 2.3.\n- **Resolution (`resolve.ts`):** when the chart's computed `color-scheme` is anything but `normal` and no `--axylo-background` decides, no `data-axylo-scheme` is written. The per-series properties were already references, not frozen colours.\n  - With `color-scheme: normal` or `--axylo-background`, it works as before.\n  - Forced colours and `prefers-contrast` are unchanged and still pass in the render suite.\n- **Canvas:** it still repaints when the change event arrives, one frame behind; a comment in `controller.ts` says so, and AX141 is judged then.\n- **Tests:**\n  - `test/theme/review.test.ts` checks the generated stylesheet.\n  - `test/browser/render/scheme.browser.test.ts` emulates the media change under `color-scheme: light dark` and reads the mark colour in the first rAF. It fails on 4504ca4.\n- **Tests changed:** three render tests and two of my chart tests expected the attribute in \"page sets color-scheme\" cases. They now expect none, which is the design change, not a loosened check.\n\n### 3. Two-tone focus ring (palette review B1)\n- **Datum ring (`core/focus.ts`):** a 4 px background-colour surround with the 2 px focus colour on top, i.e. 1 px of background on each side. Corners are closed, the ring is kept inside the plot's box, and the tooltip's avoid-box grows by the surround (`RING_REACH` 10).\n- **Plot outline (`css.ts`):** an inset `box-shadow` in the background colour under the 2 px outline. `forced-color-adjust: none` keeps it in forced colours, where both tokens are already system colours.\n- **Tests:**\n  - New browser test (`review6`): dark scheme, a bar of each of the 8 dark series colours, painted pixels at device pixel ratio 2. Crossing the ring finds background, then focus colour, then background, and the ring really crosses the neighbouring bar. It fails on 4504ca4 and passes on both renderers.\n  - `test/core/focus.test.ts`: its expectation changed from 4 focus rules to 4 surround rules plus 4 focus rules.\n- The acceptance A2 tests still pass.\n\n### 4. Untitled chart named by its target's id (React review C6)\n- `Host.targetId`, from `root.id` in `chart.ts`, is used for the diagnostic chart name and for the internal-error delivery. The allocated id stays for the DOM relationships.\n- Node tests in `snapshot.test.ts`: \"sessions\" when the target has that id, the allocated id otherwise. `test/core/host.ts` has a new `targetId` option.\n\n### Edits outside `src/core` and `src/dom`\n- `src/theme/css.ts`: the token blocks and the ring surround.\n- `src/theme/resolve.ts`: the scheme attribute rule only.\n- `test/theme/review.test.ts`, `test/browser/render/scheme.browser.test.ts` (new), and the expectations in `test/browser/render/theme.browser.test.ts` and `review.browser.test.ts`.\n- `test/browser/chart/util.ts`: `AXYLO_BUNDLE` now also reaches the render suites, without the renderer switch.\n- `palette.ts` and `test/theme/palette.test.ts` show as modified in the tree, but those are the palette author's changes, not mine.\n\n### Runs (last lines, final build)\n- **Type check:** clean.\n  - In the chained run it failed once with `packages/react/src/index.ts(114,50): error TS2554`, while the React author was editing that file.\n  - Re-run afterwards: `tsc -p packages/axylo --noEmit && tsc -p docs/api && tsc -p packages/react && tsc -p tests/dist`, no errors.\n- **Node suite without acceptance:** `Test Files 2 failed | 44 passed (46)`, `Tests 2 failed | 791 passed (793)`. Neither failure is mine:\n  - the kept memory test (`data/streaming-storage`);\n  - `scene/scene.test.ts` \"keeps generated geometry finite and clipped across independent chart states\", which fails identically on a clean HEAD tree (2727a30).\n- **My browser directories, SVG:** `Test Files 14 passed (14)`, `Tests 193 passed (193)`.\n- **My browser directories, canvas:** `Test Files 14 passed (14)`, `Tests 193 passed (193)`.\n- **Render (browser):** `Test Files 6 passed | 1 skipped (7)`, `Tests 86 passed | 1 skipped (87)`.\n- **Theme (Node):** `Test Files 3 passed (3)`, `Tests 52 passed (52)`.\n- **Browser acceptance, SVG:** `Test Files 13 passed (13)`, `Tests 555 passed (555)`.\n\nLogs are `s3-*.txt` in `/tmp/claude-1000/-home-canarit--claude-spares-spare-6ClT4i/23402a37-81bb-469c-a14a-8bf7f048d066/scratchpad/core-fix/`.\n\n<!-- Author: Claude Opus 5.5 (requested effort high), Agent-tool subagent a8a13825a6a59c62a. Saved by the coordinator from the hand-back. -->\n\n## Tree-shaking work package: report\n\nNothing is committed. `dist` was last built from the final tree, which type-checks. I made one restructuring with no intended behaviour change. All four steps are done. I ran full verification twice: at the end of step 1, and once more on the finished tree. Steps 2, 3 and 4 were verified together (Node tests at each change; the browser suites only on the final build), not one step at a time.\n\n### Baseline (measured by me at the start; the brief's 99.47 KiB predates commit e9449a6)\nMeasured from the packed tarballs with `scripts/size.mjs`:\n- everything: 107,960 B gzip (105.43 KiB)\n- line only: 107,835 B (105.31 KiB)\n- script tag: 108,088 + 5,094 CSS = 110.53 KiB\n- React adapter: 1,671 B (1.63 KiB)\n\nAt baseline the other kinds alone were about 107.6 KB each. That figure is from a source rebuild, not the tarballs, because nothing was shaken.\n\n### Step 1: per-chart composition\n**Map for a reviewer:**\n- `src/core/composition.ts` defines the `Composition` interface: normalize, openStore, view, scene, lookup, reader parts, interaction parts, and SVG and canvas mark handlers.\n- `src/kinds/{line,area,bar,scatter,donut}.ts` are plain object literals of function references. `src/kinds/index.ts` holds `COMPOSITIONS`.\n- `index.ts`: `line()` calls `createChart(LINE, …)`.\n- `core/chart.ts`: `createChart(composition, …)` uses `svgRendererWith` / `canvasRendererWith(composition.*)` and `createInteraction(composition.interaction, …)`.\n- `core/controller.ts`: new `controllerFor(composition, …)`. `createController(kind, …)` is kept for the tests and maps through `COMPOSITIONS`.\n\n**Per-shape entry points (the old dispatchers are kept as thin wrappers for tests and for `validate`):**\n- `data/normalize.ts`: `normalizeAligned`, `normalizePoints`, `normalizeDonut`. The stores and `reader/x.ts` now call these directly.\n- `data/store.ts`: `openAlignedStore`.\n- `data/store-unaligned.ts`: `openPointStore`, `openDonutStore`.\n- `view/index.ts`: `buildAlignedView`, `buildScatterView`, `buildDonutView`.\n- `scene/index.ts`: `cartesianScene` plus `line`/`area`/`bar`/`scatter` layers and scenes, and `donutScene`.\n- `lookup/index.ts`: `*LookupFor` functions.\n- `reader`: `readerWith(parts)`, with `alignedReader`, `scatterReader` and `donutReader`; the table is split per shape.\n- `core/focus.ts`: `overlayWith(targetMarks)`, with aligned, scatter and donut target marks.\n- `dom/interaction.ts`: `InteractionParts` (overlay, swatch, `stepInSeries`, `donutSliceList`).\n- `dom/swatch.ts`: `swatchPattern`, so bar pattern drawing is reached only from `barSwatch`.\n- `render/svg.ts` and `render/canvas.ts`: per-mark handler tables. `createSvgRenderer` and `createCanvasRenderer` still have every handler.\n\n**How the ESM ships:** I kept one prebundled file per condition, and made it tree-shakeable.\n- I measured both options. With all import-time work removed, prebundled and a module graph (one file per source module, `sideEffects` false) came out within 25 B for every kind.\n- The module graph was 5.7 KB better for `validate` alone, but only while the stylesheet template still did work at import.\n- I chose prebundled because it leaves `exports`, `files`, the declarations and the package-shape tests unchanged.\n\n**Proof that it shakes:** I removed every piece of import-time work:\n- marker polygons are now built lazily;\n- computed constants (2π, 2⁻²⁰, squares) became literals;\n- the registry became plain data, with the message composed in `createDiagnostic`;\n- the option-name lists are written out instead of spread;\n- `Intl.Segmenter` is created on first use;\n- the time-interval table is built lazily;\n- `TOKEN_BINDINGS` is no longer `Object.freeze`d (readonly by type only; nothing mutates it);\n- the stylesheet is a literal (step 2).\n\n**New test `tests/dist/tree-shaking.test.ts` (11 tests, all pass):**\n- Importing `axylo` only for its effects, with `ignoreAnnotations` set so esbuild ignores `sideEffects`, bundles to 0 bytes.\n- For each kind, the bundle must contain its own markers and none of the other kinds'. The markers survive minification:\n  - each kind: `kind:\"<k>\",normalize:`\n  - area: `.upper`\n  - bar: `axylo-pattern-`\n  - scatter: `.minX`\n  - donut: `__other__`\n  - aligned storage: `adoptNormalized`, which must be absent from scatter and donut\n- Each marker is first required in its own kind's bundle, so a marker lost to a rename fails the test instead of passing everything.\n\n**Sizes at the end of step 1** (source rebuilt with the release settings, before CSS minification and instrumentation removal): everything 109,948, line 92,878, area 94,874, bar 94,086, scatter 88,066, donut 81,106.\n\n### Step 2: stylesheet minified once\n- New `src/theme/minify.ts` removes only comments and layout whitespace. It keeps commas, colons, values and strings, and drops the `;` before `}`.\n- `scripts/build.mjs` substitutes the minified literal for `theme/css.ts` in `axylo.js` and `axylo.iife.min.js`, and writes the same text to `dist/axylo.css`.\n- Development files keep `css.ts` as written. I checked that `axylo.dev.js` is byte-identical to a plain `__DEV__: 'true'` rebuild, which the docs guard needs.\n- `build.mjs` now exports `stylesheets()`, `stylesheetPlugin()`, `PRODUCTION` and `DEVELOPMENT`, and only builds when run directly.\n- Stylesheet: 19,977 B → 13,917 B; gzip 5,094 B → 2,886 B.\n- New `packages/axylo/test/theme/minify.test.ts` parses both forms into the same rule tree in order (more than 50 rules and 150 declarations), checks that property names match, and tests idempotence and edge cases.\n- The licence test's origin rule now accepts \"generated from X through Y\". `THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md` was updated to match.\n\n### Step 3: production instrumentation removed\n- New `src/core/instrument.ts` is the one place for the `__bench` and `__dev` hooks, `StageCounter`, the `Probe` interface and `describe()` (with the line `kept` indexes).\n- The harness's future needs (run starts, the plot rectangle, owned bytes) go in `Probe` and `attachHooks` there.\n- The controller guards with `__DEV__ || (typeof __BENCH__ !== 'undefined' && __BENCH__)`. The `typeof` keeps the acceptance `production.test.ts` working, since it defines only `__DEV__`.\n- `__BENCH__` is declared in `core/globals.d.ts` and is false in every shipped file.\n- AX199 recovery is untouched.\n- Every browser suite loads the dev builds (`axylo.iife.js` and the dev bundles), which still have the hooks. Production-page tests already waited by frames, so no test was changed.\n- Saving: about 650–720 B gzip.\n\n**The define the benchmark build needs:**\n- Its production IIFE: `define: { __DEV__: 'false', __BENCH__: 'true' }`. `__bench` is attached when `globalThis.__AXYLO_BENCH__ === true`, as before.\n- To match shipped bytes otherwise, also add `plugins: [stylesheetPlugin(minified)]`, imported from `scripts/build.mjs`.\n- Its dev IIFE needs no change.\n- Its \"byte-identical to `scripts/build.mjs`\" check can only hold for an uninstrumented copy built with `PRODUCTION` plus the plugin.\n\n### Step 4: exact duplicates\n- `setAttr`: one copy in new `dom/attributes.ts`, used by figure, toolbar, table and interaction. `table.ts`'s variant skipped the `hasAttribute` check; removing an absent attribute is a no-op, so behaviour is the same.\n- `clamp`: `lookup/aligned` now uses `scene/geometry`'s.\n- `withUnit`: `core/axes` now uses `reader/table`'s.\n- Binary search: `focus.lowerBound` and the controller's `indexOf` now use `lookup/search`.\n- Message-key allowlist: now `Object.keys(defaultMessages)`. I checked that it has the same 60 keys in the same order, which matters for suggestion ties.\n- `store-unaligned`'s `lowerBound` stays a commented copy, because `test/data/review.test.ts` forbids data importing lookup.\n\n### Final sizes (from the packed tarballs)\n| Case | Before | After |\n|---|---|---|\n| everything | 107,960 (105.43 KiB) | 106,738 (104.24 KiB) |\n| line only (first-chart npm.ts) | 107,835 | 89,605 (87.50 KiB) |\n| line export | — | 89,516 |\n| area | ~107.6 KB | 91,514 |\n| bar | ~107.6 KB | 89,997 |\n| scatter | ~107.6 KB | 83,830 |\n| donut | ~107.6 KB | 77,125 |\n| validate | ~21 KB | 20,887 |\n| script tag (iife.min + css) | 110.53 KiB | 106,833 + 2,886 = 107.15 KiB |\n| React adapter | 1.63 KiB | 1.63 KiB |\n\nI restored `bench/size-latest.json` after each `size.mjs` run. Note that `scripts/size.mjs`'s per-module breakdown rebuilds without the stylesheet plugin, so it now wrongly prints \"dist was built from different source\". `size.mjs` was outside my files; it should use `stylesheetPlugin` and `PRODUCTION` from `build.mjs`.\n\n### Last lines of every suite\n- `npm run typecheck`: clean.\n- `npx vitest run`: `Tests 1 failed | 1579 passed (1580)`. The one failure is the known `streaming-storage` memory-budget test.\n- Browser, SVG, all of `packages/axylo/test/browser`: `Test Files 33 passed | 1 skipped (34)`, `Tests 834 passed | 1 skipped (835)`. Same result at step 1 and on the final build.\n- Browser, canvas, chart and a11y: `Tests 193 passed (193)`, at step 1 and on the final build.\n- Dist, Node: `Tests 1 failed | 116 passed (117)`. The failure is `conditions.test.ts > @axylo/react > production file carries none of the development-only text`. It is not mine: I confirmed in a temporary worktree of HEAD that it fails there too, and the React build is byte-identical to HEAD's. The cause is `patch.ts`'s `{ value }` shorthand, which appears as `value:` in the minified file.\n- Dist, browser: `Tests 8 failed | 22 passed (30)`. All 8 are `csp.browser.test.ts`, failing with \"axylo.css declares no light --axylo-default-series-1\". Also not mine: they fail identically at HEAD. The test's hex regex predates the `light-dark()` tokens added in e9449a6.\n- React: `Tests 39 passed (39)` at step 1 and on the final build.\n- `apps/docs` browser: `6 failed | 1 passed` files. **This one is caused by my change.** The docs guard in `apps/docs/lib/site.mjs` (`checkSameRevision`) requires the installed `axylo.css` to equal `css.ts`'s string. The exact fix: compare against `minifyCss(AXYLO_CSS)` from `src/theme/minify.ts`. `apps/docs/lib/theme.mjs`'s `block()` also expects `selector + ' {'` and a `;` after every declaration, so it needs to accept the minified form. I did not edit `apps/docs`. The docs agent is blocked by this until it is relayed. If you would rather keep `dist/axylo.css` readable instead, that is a one-line change in `build.mjs`.\n\n### Shared code that still names other kinds (kept on purpose, all small and with no module edges)\n- The `resolveOptions` key table, `readX`'s type checks, `viewStateFor`, `KIND_NAMES` / `KIND_MESSAGES`.\n- Inline frame-shape branches in the controller: `formatSpec` with `pointDateUnit`, `pointFor`, `freeze`, `latestX`, `renamed`, `positionValue`, `hasData`, `themeKeys`.\n- Inline branches in interaction: `aligned()` checks, `stepAcross` used for initial focus, the ring hole in placement.\n- Bar bands and the stack inside the aligned view, which all three aligned kinds share.\n\nThese are the next candidates if you want more off `line`, estimated at a few hundred bytes gzip.\n\n### Things that looked wrong (not fixed)\n- The `conditions` and CSP dist test failures above, both pre-existing.\n- `controller.ts` imports `fill`, which was already unused at HEAD.\n\n### What a reviewer should look at first\n1. `src/kinds/*`: is the set of mark handlers per kind complete (scene plus overlay marks)?\n2. `view/index.ts` `cartesianBase` and the `scene/index.ts` split: is the order of diagnostics and layers the same as before?\n3. The registry rewritten as data in `diagnostics/registry.ts`.\n4. The guard expression in `controller.ts` / `instrument.ts`.\n5. `theme/minify.ts` and the docs conflict above.\n\nAll of `packages/axylo/src`, `scripts/build.mjs`, `tests/dist/licence.test.ts`, `tests/dist/tree-shaking.test.ts`, `packages/axylo/test/theme/minify.test.ts` and `THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md` are under `/home/canarit/projects/axylo`. The scratch directory has been removed.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
