src/core/contracts.ts:574:  /** Draws or clears (null) the overlay without redrawing the scene. */\npackages/axylo/src/core/contracts.ts:575:  drawOverlay(overlay: Overlay | null, bindings: PaintBindings, theme: ResolvedTheme, devicePixelRatio: number): void;\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:328:  const overlayClipId = `axylo-clip-${++ids}`;\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:330:  const overlayClip = clipPath(doc, defs, overlayClipId);\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:339:  const overlayGroup = doc.createElementNS(NS, 'g');\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:340:  overlayGroup.setAttribute('class', 'axylo-overlay');\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:341:  svg.append(defs, background.el, sceneGroup, overlayGroup);\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:385:    drawOverlay(overlay: Overlay | null, bindings: PaintBindings, _theme: ResolvedTheme, devicePixelRatio: number): void {\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:387:      overlayGroup.replaceChildren();\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:388:      if (overlay === null) return;\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:389:      setRect(overlayClip, overlay.clip);\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:390:      const url = `url(#${overlayClipId})`;\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:395:        overlayGroup.appendChild(n.el);\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:397:      for (const m of overlay.clipped) add(m, url, overlay.clip);\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:398:      for (const m of overlay.unclipped) add(m, null, null);\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:464:    // The overlay carries the series colour too, so the element painted in that colour also says which\npackages/axylo/dist\ntests/dist/fixtures/npm/node_modules/axylo/dist\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_61","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat examples/first-chart/npm.ts\ngit show a518cbb:docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md | sed -n '130,148p'\nnl -ba docs/02-product-spec.md | sed -n '235,250p'\ncat packages/axylo/tsconfig.json docs/api/tsconfig.json tests/dist/tsconfig.json\"","aggregated_output":"import { line } from 'axylo';\n\nconst rows = [\n  { month: '2026-01', visitors: 4210 },\n  { month: '2026-02', visitors: 4685 },\n  { month: '2026-03', visitors: 5302 },\n  { month: '2026-04', visitors: 5127 },\n];\n\nline('#chart', { title: 'Monthly visitors', data: rows, x: 'month', y: 'visitors' });\n### 1.6 Diagnostics\n\nThe registry, with each code's level and outcome, is in the spec (section 3.5). Example for the Phase 1 combined dirty fixture (`[10, 20, null, NaN, '40', undefined, 70]`):\n\n```\n[axylo AX103] \"Dirty input\": y field \"value\" has 1 value that is not a finite number (NaN at row 3). Shown as a gap.\n  Check the calculation that produced it, or send null for \"no data\".\n[axylo AX106] \"Dirty input\": y field \"value\" mixes numbers with 1 string (\"40\" at row 4). The string is not plotted.\n  Convert it to a number where the data is produced.\n[axylo AX102] \"Dirty input\": y field \"value\" is absent in 1 row (row 5). Shown as a gap.\n  Send null if the value is known to be missing.\n```\n\nThe reader of that chart sees \"Values not shown: 3. See table.\" (F12).\n\n`validate(type, options)` runs the same normalization without a DOM and returns the diagnostics, so a server, a test or a coding agent can check a chart before rendering it.\n\nDevelopment and production flavours are selected through package `exports` conditions. The script-tag distribution ships `axylo.js` (development) and `axylo.min.js` (production). Errors and warnings print once per code per page in both; hints only in development.\n\n   235\t## 5. Performance budgets\n   236\t\n   237\tThese numbers are provisional. They are design targets taken from Phase 1's order-of-magnitude measurements, and they become release gates only after the benchmark harness built in M0 has measured a working Axylo slice in M1 and the limits have been recalibrated and frozen (plan, M1 and M6). Each budget names its workload, metric and statistic so that two people measuring the same build get the same verdict.\n   238\t\n   239\t| ID | Workload | Metric | Statistic | Provisional limit | Phase 1 reference |\n   240\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n   241\t| P1 | `trend` (3×120), default options, reference desktop | Main-thread task time from the call until the chart is complete | Median of 20 runs | 10 ms | Not comparable across libraries below about 35 ms with the Phase 1 method |\n   242\t| P2 | One series, 100,000 and 1,000,000 points, typed columns | Same | Median of 20 | 60 ms and 250 ms | See 01, section 5.3: uPlot and tuned Chart.js are the fastest measured |\n   243\t| P3 | 600-point window, one appended point per display frame at 60 Hz, driven by an independent clock | Main-thread task time per update (per run: task time in the window divided by the number of updates); and frames later than 25 ms | Median over runs; count per 300 frames | 2 ms; 0 | See 01, section 5.3 |\n   244\t| P4 | Pointer moved across a 100,000-point chart | Time from input to the frame showing the matching readout | 95th percentile | One frame (16.7 ms at 60 Hz) | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n   245\t| P5 | 24 `trend` charts created on one page, each tile 340 × 200 px | Total main-thread task time | Median of 20 | 200 ms | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n   246\t| P6 | Download | Minified and gzipped bytes, CSS included | Exact | DX7 | 01, section 1.4 |\n   247\t| P8 | 40 compact charts created on one page, each 160 × 48 px with one series | Total main-thread task time | Median of 20 | 80 ms | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n   248\t| P7 | Memory | Bytes owned by Axylo beyond the caller's columns for 1,000,000 points; heap trend over 1,000 create–destroy cycles | Measured after forced collection | At most 1× the size of the caller's two columns; no upward slope | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n   249\t\n   250\tThree rules hold whatever the numbers become:\n{ \"extends\": \"../../tsconfig.base.json\", \"compilerOptions\": { \"rootDir\": \".\", \"outDir\": \"dist/types\" }, \"include\": [\"src\", \"test\"] }\n{ \"compilerOptions\": { \"strict\": true, \"noEmit\": true, \"target\": \"ES2022\", \"module\": \"ESNext\", \"moduleResolution\": \"Bundler\", \"lib\": [\"ES2022\", \"DOM\"], \"skipLibCheck\": false }, \"include\": [\"examples.ts\", \"../../packages/axylo/src/core/globals.d.ts\"] }\n{\n  \"extends\": \"../../tsconfig.base.json\",\n  \"compilerOptions\": { \"noEmit\": true, \"types\": [\"node\"] },\n  \"include\": [\"*.ts\", \"pages/*.ts\"]\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_62","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat docs/decisions/0001-plot-interaction-pattern.md docs/decisions/0002-mark-renderer.md\nrg -n '__renderer|canvas' docs/02-product-spec.md docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md docs/api packages/axylo/README.md scripts/build.mjs packages/axylo/src/core/chart.ts\nrg -n 'distinct|hash|case' packages/axylo/src/theme/resolve.ts | head -28\nls -l packages/axylo/dist/axylo.js tests/dist/fixtures/npm/node_modules/axylo/dist/axylo.js\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_62","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat docs/decisions/0001-plot-interaction-pattern.md docs/decisions/0002-mark-renderer.md\nrg -n '__renderer|canvas' docs/02-product-spec.md docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md docs/api packages/axylo/README.md scripts/build.mjs packages/axylo/src/core/chart.ts\nrg -n 'distinct|hash|case' packages/axylo/src/theme/resolve.ts | head -28\nls -l packages/axylo/dist/axylo.js tests/dist/fixtures/npm/node_modules/axylo/dist/axylo.js\"","aggregated_output":"# Decision 0001: how the plot is exposed to keyboards and screen readers\n\nStatus: working choice, **not rehearsed with any screen reader**. The plan (M1) called for three candidate patterns to be rehearsed on five screen-reader and browser pairs before choosing. No screen reader can be run in the environment this was built in, so the choice below was made by reasoning and by inspecting Chrome's accessibility tree, and it must be rehearsed by the owner before it is relied on. If rehearsal contradicts it, the plot's roles are internal and can change without touching the public API.\n\n## Candidates\n\n| Pattern | For | Against |\n|---|---|---|\n| A. `role=\"application\"` on the plot, arrow keys handled in script, point text sent to a live region | Used by Recharts and ApexCharts. Full control of keys. | Screen-reader authors discourage `application`; a live region is a second, slower channel; touch screen readers have no arrow keys, so points cannot be reached by swiping. |\n| B. A roving focusable proxy element per point (or one moved around), each with its own label | Each point is a real element with a name. | One element per point does not scale to 100,000 points; a single moved proxy re-announces its role on every move in some readers (from memory, not verified). |\n| C. One native `<input type=\"range\">` laid over the plot, its value the position along x, its `aria-valuetext` the point's text | A native control: desktop readers enter focus mode on it by themselves, arrow keys, Home, End and Page keys work without script, and a change of value text is read without a live region. Touch screen readers expose a native range as an adjustable element, so swiping up and down moves through points (from memory for VoiceOver on iOS and TalkBack; not verified here). One element whatever the number of points. | It is announced as a slider, which is not what a chart is; mitigated by `aria-roledescription` and a label that says what moving it does. Up and Down natively do the same as Right and Left. |\n\n## Choice: C\n\nOne visually transparent native range input covers the plot. It is the plot's single tab stop.\n\n- **Value**: the ordinal of the current x among the visible x positions (for scatter: among visible points in x then y order; for donut: slice order), from `Lookup.ordinal` and `Lookup.count`.\n- **Value text**: by default everything at that x, like the pointer tooltip: the x, then each visible series with its value, then \"point 4 of 120\". After the reader narrows to one series (Up or Down on a keyboard), only that series is read. Escape returns to all series.\n- **Keys**: Left and Right move along x (native). Home, End, Page Up and Page Down native. Up and Down are intercepted to change the series in focus. `+`, `-`, `0` and Shift+arrows zoom and pan. Enter or Space selects (fires `select`). Escape: closes the tooltip first, then leaves single-series mode; otherwise it is not consumed, so a chart inside a dialog does not swallow it.\n- **Touch screen readers**: swiping up or down on the adjustable element moves along x and reads every series at that x; the table is one control away in the toolbar.\n- **Everything else** (legend toggles, zoom buttons, pause, table) is ordinary buttons in one toolbar with roving focus: the chart's second and last tab stop while the table is closed.\n\n## What has to be checked by rehearsal\n\n1. NVDA with Chrome and with Firefox: focus mode is entered on the range without a keystroke; each arrow press reads the new value text once; Up and Down are passed through and the series change is read.\n2. VoiceOver with Safari on macOS: the same, and the role description is read sensibly.\n3. VoiceOver on iOS and TalkBack with Chrome: the element is reachable by touch exploration at the plot's position; swipe up and down move by one point and read it; the toolbar and table are reachable by swiping right.\n4. That a long value text (eight series) is not cut off, and that streaming data does not make the reader speak unless the reader moves.\n# Decision 0002: SVG paths draw the marks by default (provisional)\n\nStatus: **provisional working default**, set by the coordinator on 2026-11-18 from the M1 measurements and weakened the same day after an independent review of the benchmark harness (`reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/bench.review.md`, Codex gpt-6.2-sol at effort max). The owner asked for this to be settled by measurement. It is not settled: the measurements support using SVG while the line-chart slice is built and keeping canvas behind the internal switch. They do not establish a shipping winner or a reason to remove either renderer.\n\n## What the review changed\n\nThe table below is kept as measured. These statements made from it in the first version are withdrawn or narrowed:\n\n- **Withdrawn: \"nothing measured favours canvas except all-thread CPU during resize.\"** The recorded heap, DOM-node and layout counts also favour canvas.\n- **Withdrawn: the resize CPU gap is raster work.** The number is CPU for the whole renderer process; no stage was traced.\n- **Withdrawn: simplification bounds every chart by its width.** It was shown for line series only; point marks and many-series charts were not measured.\n- **Withdrawn: an SVG theme change needs no run-time work.** Both renderers recorded six DOM mutations per toggle; \"no canvas drawing call\" is all that was observed.\n- **Withdrawn: normalization and garbage collection explain the gap to uPlot.** About 71–73% of busy time in the production profiles is unmapped; the normalization share comes from development builds. It is a hypothesis to test, not a finding.\n- **Narrowed: \"half the renderer memory.\"** The halving is of the increase above a data-only page at pixel ratio 2 (34.2 → 17.7 MiB). Absolute renderer memory was 74.7 → 56.2 MiB, and at pixel ratio 1 the increase was 23.1 → 18.4 MiB.\n- **Narrowed: \"no frame later than 25 ms.\"** True of the recorded animation-frame callbacks; the window can miss an interval that spans its boundary, and a callback is not a presented frame.\n- **Void: every 24-chart page row** (page at pixel ratio 1 and 2, resize of the 24-chart page, and the memory row). The harness fix pass found that these runs drew no marks: at 230 × 115 px each tile is below the 120 × 80 px plot minimum of spec L8, so every chart showed its text fallback. The SVG records carry `svgPaths: 0`; the canvas runs held empty canvases. The numbers compare two empty charts and say nothing about the renderers. The harness now rejects a run whose chart drew no marks, and the page workload uses 340 × 200 px tiles.\n- **Narrowed: verdicts.** 149 metric comparisons were judged without adjustment for choosing among them, on six pairs or fewer per workload, with noise bands from one session and two workload shapes. Two \"SVG lower\" rows allow a gain as small as 0.16 ms (10,000 points) and 6.3 ms (dashboard at pixel ratio 2).\n\nEvidence: [bench/results/m1-renderer-comparison.md](../../bench/results/m1-renderer-comparison.md). One session of 39 runs, production build, headless Chrome 157, machine under load from other work, A-versus-A noise bands for two workloads only.\n\n## What was measured\n\nCanvas → SVG, medians of main-thread task time unless stated. \"Lower\" means the 95% interval cleared zero and the noise band where one existed.\n\n| Workload | Canvas | SVG | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Streaming, per update | 2.26 ms | 1.45 ms | SVG lower |\n| Theme change, per toggle | 2.68 ms and 27 drawing calls | 2.01 ms and no drawing call | SVG lower |\n| Resize, one chart of 100,000 points | 915 ms | 472 ms | SVG lower on the main thread |\n| Same, CPU across all threads | 1,035 ms | 1,770 ms | **SVG higher** |\n| Resize, 24-chart page | 730 ms | 525 ms | Void: no marks were drawn |\n| First chart, 10,000 points | 34.0 ms | 28.4 ms | SVG lower |\n| 24-chart page at pixel ratio 2 | 199 ms | 158 ms | Void: no marks were drawn |\n| First chart, 3 × 120 | 23.6 ms | 19.8 ms | Inconclusive |\n| First chart, 100,000 points | 81.8 ms | 83.5 ms | Inconclusive |\n| First chart, 1,000,000 points | 622 ms | 652 ms | Inconclusive (4 blocks) |\n| Memory, 24-chart page at pixel ratio 2 | 34.2 MiB renderer, 11.5 MiB GPU, 7.7 MiB backing stores | 17.7 MiB, 2.2 MiB, none | Void: no marks were drawn |\n\nNo frame was later than 25 ms in any configuration, so frame pacing does not separate the two.\n\n## Why SVG for now\n\n1. On the line workloads measured, main-thread task time was lower with SVG or the two could not be told apart.\n2. Line series are simplified to a few vertices per pixel column before either renderer sees them, so for line charts the path size follows the plot width, not the data.\n3. SVG follows the page with less code: forced colors and print work through CSS, browser zoom stays sharp, and there is no backing store to size, cap or lose. This is a design argument, not a measurement.\n\n## What this does not establish\n\n- **GPU rasterization was not measured.** Headless Chrome here does not use a GPU. The resize, pixel-ratio-2 and process-memory results need a headed, GPU-backed rerun before they transfer.\n- **Dense point marks were not measured.** A scatter chart with tens of thousands of markers is the case most likely to favour canvas.\n- **One browser, one machine, one session.** Firefox and Safari may order the two differently.\n- **Many series, pan and zoom, and the budget workloads P4, P7 and P8 were not measured**, and the million-point comparison has four pairs.\n- Both renderers are far from uPlot in the configurations measured (about 80 ms against 12 ms at 100,000 points; uPlot draws no tooltip or accessibility layer). Where that time goes is not established.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- The working default is SVG. The canvas renderer stays in the code behind the same internal switch, with the pixel-agreement tests between the two kept.\n- Before the choice is final: fix the harness defects the review found (renderer identity in the readback, noise bands on replay, failed runs excluded from speed verdicts, gap-aware admission, the frame window), declare the primary endpoints and thresholds in advance, then rerun on a quiet machine with scatter at 10,000, 50,000 and 200,000 points, ten series of 100,000 points, pan and zoom, and the byte cost of keeping both renderers. Removing canvas from the bundle needs that evidence too.\n- When a real GPU and a second browser are available: rerun the resize and million-point workloads headed. If canvas wins there by more than the shipping thresholds in the performance plan, flip the switch back.\n- The plan's sections 2.2 and 2.3 are read with SVG in the \"marks\" position: a theme change no longer needs a redraw, and device pixel ratio matters only to simplification.\npackages/axylo/src/core/chart.ts:15:import { canvasRendererWith } from '../render/canvas';\npackages/axylo/src/core/chart.ts:65:    const requested = object(input).__renderer === 'canvas' ? 'canvas' : 'svg';\npackages/axylo/src/core/chart.ts:68:    const renderer: MarkRenderer = requested === 'canvas' ? canvasRendererWith(composition.canvas, doc) : svgRendererWith(composition.svg, doc);\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:210:**This is reopened and decided in M1 by measurement.** The first draft justified canvas by Phase 1's slow SVG libraries, but those create a node per point; the one-path-per-series SVG chart in the harness was fast. With drawing simplified to a few vertices per pixel column (F5), an SVG path per series is small. SVG follows CSS variables and class-toggled themes with no repaint code, adapts to forced colors, prints sharply, needs no pixel-ratio handling and allocates no backing store (a 1,920×1,080 canvas at pixel ratio 2 is about 33 MB). Canvas is likely cheaper for per-frame redraw when streaming and hovering.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:393:| DOM text costs too much with many charts on a page. | P5 is measured in M1. A fallback to canvas-drawn tick labels would give up user text spacing and forced-colors adaptation for those labels, so it must pass the same L1 and A6 tests before it is allowed. |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:270:| Canvas marks with DOM text | D9: a canvas library is the speed reference. D10: one DOM node per point is slow. | **Reopened.** Phase 1 does not show that SVG is slow, only that one node per point is; a single SVG path per series was fast. M1 builds both mark renderers on the same scene and measures them (section 8). Canvas is the working assumption. |\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 canarit canarit 303903 Nov 18 11:43 packages/axylo/dist/axylo.js\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 canarit canarit 303903 Nov 18 11:44 tests/dist/fixtures/npm/node_modules/axylo/dist/axylo.js\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_63","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"node --expose-gc - <<'AXYLO_REVIEW_STDIN'\nconst { buildSync, build } = require('esbuild');\nconst { execFileSync } = require('node:child_process');\nconst path = require('node:path');\nconst assert = require('node:assert/strict');\nconst cwd = process.cwd();\nfunction load(source, define = {__DEV__:'true'}, extra = {}) {\n  const b = buildSync({stdin:{contents:source,loader:'ts',resolveDir:cwd},bundle:true,write:false,format:'cjs',platform:'node',target:'es2022',define,logLevel:'silent',...extra});\n  return evaluate(b.outputFiles[0].text);\n}\nfunction evaluate(js){const m={exports:{}};new Function('module','exports','require',js)(m,m.exports,require);return m.exports;}\nconst fromHost=\\\"export {record,lastModel} from './packages/axylo/test/core/host';\\\";\nconst fromPublic=\\\"export {validate} from './packages/axylo/src/index';\\\";\nconst opts=(data,extra={})=>({title:'Review',data,x:'x',y:'y',...extra});\nconst rows=(xs)=>xs.map(x=>({x,y:x*10,z:x*100}));\nconst kinds=['line','area','bar','scatter','donut'];\nfunction hover(r,ref){let out;const off=r.controller.handle.on('hover',v=>out=v);r.controller.actions.hover(ref);off();return out;}\nfunction alignedXs(r){return [...r.committed.view.frame.x.values];}\nfunction hoverX(r,x,key='y'){return hover(r,{kind:'aligned',seriesKey:key,x});}\nfunction codes(c){return c.diagnostics.map(d=>d.code+(d.field?':'+d.field:''));}\n\nconst {record,lastModel}=load(fromHost);\n{\n  let chart, fired=0;\n  const f=v=>{if(chart&&v>=50&&\"'!fired++){chart.update({data:rows([10,12])});}return String(v);};\n  const r=record('\"'line',opts(rows([0,2]),{title:'First',y:{field:'y',format:f}}));\n  chart=r.controller.handle;r.frames();const count=r.draws.length;\n  chart.update({title:'Intermediate',data:rows([5,7])});r.frames(1);\n  assert.equal(lastModel(r).title.text,'First');assert.deepEqual(alignedXs(r),[0,2]);assert.equal(r.draws.